{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AmmkHDYAYhbpen8d3jA8WT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Chu & Stuckey's (revised) algorithm\n2\t\n3\tSection 4 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md` §4) in paper form. It distils\n4\tthe working report `search_soundness.md` (Ralph loop0006, items 05 to 13),\n5\twhich holds the code references, the full check tables and the history. Every\n6\tstatement here is proved in Lean unless it says otherwise. The Lean names\n7\trefer to `../lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`, namespace\n8\t`MOSPFormalization.Search`: nine files (`Basic`, `DefiniteMove`,\n9\t`SubsetRule`, `BetterMove`, `Memo`, `Decide`, `DefiniteMatching`,\n10\t`PublishedTheorems`, `ChuThesis`), with no\n11\t`sorry` and no axioms beyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`\n12\t(`axiom_check.lean`). Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the\n13\tpreprint `../literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`. Page numbers for Chu (2011),\n14\tChu's PhD thesis, which restates the search in its chapter 6, are the printed\n15\tones of `../literature/chu_2011_phd_thesis_improving_combinatorial_optimization.pdf`.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## 4.1 Introduction\n20\t\n21\tLet $M$ be a MOSP instance and $k \\ge 0$ an integer. The algorithm of this\n22\tsection decides whether some sequence of the products keeps at most $k$\n23\tcustomer stacks open at once, that is, whether $Z(M) \\le k$. By (E2) of\n24\tsection 3 (Lean: `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`), $Z(M) \\le k$ holds exactly\n25\twhen the MOSP graph $G_M$ has pathwidth at most $k - 1$. So the algorithm is\n26\talso a decision procedure for pathwidth. Any graph $G$ is the MOSP graph of the\n27\tinstance with one customer per vertex and one product per edge, each product\n28\tneeded by the two ends of its edge.\n29\t\n30\tThe algorithm is the *customer search* of Chu & Stuckey (2009). It searches\n31\tover the orders in which the customers' stacks close, not over product\n32\tsequences, and it prunes the search with several dominance rules. Chu &\n33\tStuckey state three of these rules as theorems (their Theorems 1, 2 and 3) and\n34\ta fourth, the subset rule, in their §2.\n35\t\n36\t**Two of the published theorems are false as stated.** Theorem 1, the\n37\t*definite move*, can discard the last solution at a node. A 14-customer graph\n38\tshows it, and Lean proves the counterexample. Theorem 2, the *better move*,\n39\trests on Theorem 1 applied one step deeper, and it inherits the fault. Its\n40\tform as the code ran it from 2026-09-26 to 2026-10-01 is still false. Chu's\n41\tthesis (Chu 2011, §6.3) restates both: its Theorem 6.3.6 is Theorem 1\n42\tverbatim, and its Theorem 6.3.8 is the better move with a different premise.\n43\tThe same graph refutes both, in Lean.\n44\t\n45\t**The revised algorithm** keeps the search and every rule. It replaces the\n46\tpremises of Theorems 1 and 2 by a stronger, *hereditary* premise. That premise\n47\tis proved sound, and it is proved equivalent to a bipartite matching\n48\tcondition, so it costs one small matching per candidate. With the two repairs,\n49\tthe whole search is proved sound in Lean: a refutation at $k$ means\n50\t$Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$.\n51\t\n52\tNo wrong answer of the unrepaired search on a whole instance is known. The\n53\tgap is at the level of a node, and the search has always recovered through\n54\tanother branch.\n55\t\n56\t**The owner's decision** (2026-10-01, `plan.md`, *Decisions*), now carried\n57\tout. This paper states the gap and the repaired rule. Both solvers, the MOSP\n58\tcustomer search and the graph pathwidth solver, apply the repaired rules by\n59\tdefault since 2026-10-01 (Ralph loop0007, `solver_fix.md`), and the published\n60\trules remain behind a flag. Of the 115 corpus values that rested on the\n61\tcustomer search alone, 108 have been re-refuted by the repaired solver and none\n62\tchanged; the other 7 need a longer run. Section 4.4.4 states what holds for the\n63\tcode, and section 4.6.3 what is left.\n64\t\n65\t---\n66\t\n67\t## 4.2 The search as a mathematical object\n68\t\n69\t### 4.2.1 Definitions\n70\t\n71\tChu & Stuckey observe that the products matter only through the MOSP graph:\n72\t\"all functions c(p) that produce the same customer graph have the same minimum\n73\tnumber of stacks\" (PDF p. 4). So fix a finite simple graph $G = (C, E)$ whose\n74\tvertices are the customers, and an integer $k \\ge 0$. For a customer $c$ and a\n75\tset $T \\subseteq C$, write\n76\t\n77\t- $N[c] = \\{c\\} \\cup \\{d : cd \\in E\\}$, the closed neighbourhood: the stacks\n78\t  that must be open when $c$'s stack closes;\n79\t- $O(T) = \\bigcup_{c \\in T} N[c]$, the stacks *opened* once the customers of\n80\t  $T$ have closed, with $O(\\emptyset) = \\emptyset$;\n81\t- $\\mathrm{fin}(X) = \\{c : N[c] \\subseteq X\\}$, the customers that could close\n82\t  at no cost once the stacks $X$ are open;\n83\t- $\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{fin}(O(T))$, the *free closure* of $T$;\n84\t- $b(T) = |O(T) \\setminus T|$, the stacks open after $T$ has closed;\n85\t- $o(c, T) = N[c] \\setminus O(T)$, the stacks that closing $c$ next would newly\n86\t  open, and $\\mathrm{open}(c, T) = |o(c, T)|$.\n87\t\n88\tTwo facts are immediate: $T \\subseteq \\mathrm{cl}(T)$ and\n89\t$O(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) = O(T)$ (Lean: `subset_cl`, `opened_cl`). The second says\n90\tthat free closing opens nothing.\n91\t\n92\t**Moves and costs.** A *move* at $T$ closes one customer $c \\notin T$. Its cost\n93\tis the number of stacks open at that moment, counting $c$ itself:\n94\t$$\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) = |O(T \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus T| = b(T \\cup \\{c\\}) + 1.$$\n95\tThis is Chu & Stuckey's $|O(S) - S \\cup o(c, S)|$ (PDF p. 4). The move is\n96\t*playable* when $\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) \\le k$. A *closing order from* $T$ is an\n97\tenumeration $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ of $C \\setminus T$. Its cost is the largest of\n98\tits step costs $\\mathrm{cost}(T \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_{i-1}\\}, c_i)$, and $0$\n99\twhen $m = 0$. Write $P_k(T)$ when some closing order from $T$ costs at most\n100\t$k$. (Lean: `stepCost`, `orderCost`, `Solvable`;\n101\t`stepCost_eq_openStacks_insert_add_one` for the second form of the cost.)\n102\t\n103\t**States.** On entering a node, the search closes every customer whose\n104\tneighbourhood is already open. One pass suffices, because closing such a\n105\tcustomer opens nothing. So the search only ever stands at a *state*, a set\n106\t$S$ with $S = \\mathrm{cl}(S)$. The root $\\emptyset$ is a state, since every\n107\t$N[c]$ is nonempty. The *child* of a state $S$ by $c \\notin S$ is\n108\t$$S \\cdot c = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{c\\}).$$\n109\t\n110\t**The decision predicate.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ holds when $S = C$, or when some\n111\t$c \\notin S$ has $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$ and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n112\tThis is the question the search answers: \"is there a run of playable moves,\n113\teach followed by its free moves, that closes everyone?\" (Lean: `SearchSol`,\n114\tdefined inductively and not through $P_k$, so that the link between the two is\n115\ta theorem.)\n116\t\n117\t**The invariant.** Every node the search visits satisfies\n118\t$$b(S) = |O(S) \\setminus S| \\le k.$$\n119\tIt holds at the root, where $b(\\emptyset) = 0$. At a child it holds because\n120\t$b(S \\cdot c) \\le |O(S \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{c\\})| =\n121\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$ (Lean: `card_opened_insert_sdiff_lt`). Several\n122\tresults below need it, and it is never an extra assumption about the search.\n123\t\n124\t### 4.2.2 Lemma F: free moves never hurt\n125\t\n126\t**Lemma 4.1 (monotonicity).** If $T \\subseteq T'$ and $O(T') \\subseteq O(T)$,\n127\tthen $P_k(T) \\Rightarrow P_k(T')$.\n128\t\n129\t*Proof.* Take a closing order from $T$ of cost at most $k$ and delete the\n130\tcustomers of $T'$ from it. Each remaining step closes some $c$ at a prefix\n131\t$T' \\cup A$ in place of $T \\cup A$. Its opened set\n132\t$O(T' \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\}) \\subseteq O(T \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\})$ is no larger, and the\n133\tset removed from it, $T' \\cup A \\supseteq T \\cup A$, is no smaller. So no step\n134\tcosts more. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_mono`, via `orderCost_filter_le`.)\n135\t\n136\t**Lemma 4.2 (Lemma F).** If $b(T) \\le k$, then\n137\t$P_k(T) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$. The direction ($\\Rightarrow$)\n138\tneeds no hypothesis. At the root, $P_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$.\n139\t\n140\t*Proof.* ($\\Rightarrow$) By Lemma 4.1 with $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T)$, it suffices\n141\tto show $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ for a state $S$. Induct on\n142\t$|C \\setminus S|$. If $S \\ne C$, let $c$ be the first move of an order from\n143\t$S$ of cost at most $k$. Then $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$, the rest of the\n144\torder gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, Lemma 4.1 gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$, and\n145\tinduction gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n146\t\n147\t($\\Leftarrow$) First, $P_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) \\Rightarrow P_k(T)$ when\n148\t$b(T) \\le k$. Close the customers of $\\mathrm{cl}(T) \\setminus T$ one at a\n149\ttime. Each step at a prefix $U$ with $T \\subseteq U$ costs\n150\t$|O(T) \\setminus U| \\le b(T) \\le k$, because the customer closed lies in\n151\t$O(T)$ already. Then follow the order from $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n152\tSecond, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(S)$ at a state with the invariant,\n153\tby induction on $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. At a move $c$, the child $S \\cdot c$\n154\tsatisfies the invariant, so induction gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$. The first step\n155\tthen gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, because $b(S \\cup \\{c\\}) =\n156\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$. Prepending $c$, at cost at most $k$, gives\n157\t$P_k(S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_searchSol_cl`,\n158\t`searchSol_cl_of_solvable`, `solvable_of_searchSol`, `solvable_cl_iff`;\n159\tat the root, `solvable_empty_iff`.)\n160\t\n161\tThe hypothesis is needed. On $K_2$ with one customer closed and $k = 0$,\n162\t$\\mathrm{cl}(T)$ is everything, so $\\mathrm{Sol}_0(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$ holds, but\n163\tclosing the other customer costs one stack.\n164\t\n165\t*In plain English.* Once every neighbour of a customer has an open stack, that\n166\tcustomer can close straight away without opening anything new. Doing so never\n167\tmakes later steps dearer, and it makes some cheaper, because one fewer stack\n168\tis left open. So a search that always closes such customers first loses\n169\tnothing. The only proviso is that the stacks already open must fit within the\n170\tbudget, since closing the free customers one by one costs exactly that many.\n171\t\n172\t### 4.2.3 Cost is narrowness, so the search decides pathwidth\n173\t\n174\tFor a full closing order $\\tau = c_1, \\dots, c_n$ from $\\emptyset$, write\n175\t$S_i = \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$ and\n176\t$\\partial X = \\{v \\notin X : v \\text{ has a neighbour in } X\\}$. The $i$-th step\n177\tcosts\n178\t$$|O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}| = 1 + |\\partial S_i|,$$\n179\tbecause $O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}$ is $c_i$ together with the customers\n180\toutside $S_i$ that have a neighbour in $S_i$. The maximum of these numbers over\n181\t$i$ is the *narrowness of the sequence* $\\tau$, in the dual shack process of\n182\tKornai & Tuza (1992), formalised as `Complex.outNarrowness`.\n183\t\n184\t**Theorem 4.3 (the search decides pathwidth and MOSP).** For nonempty $C$,\n185\t$$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\min_\\tau \\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) \\le k \\iff \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 \\le k.$$\n186\tFor a MOSP instance $M$ with at least one requirement, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$\n187\ton $G_M$ holds iff $Z(M) \\le k$.\n188\t\n189\t*Proof.* The first equivalence is Lemma 4.2 at the root. For the second, read\n190\t$\\tau$ backwards as a layout $L$, so that the cut after position $n - i$ of\n191\t$L$ separates $C \\setminus S_i$ (left) from $S_i$ (right). The left vertices\n192\twith a right neighbour are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So\n193\t$\\max_{1 \\le i < n} |\\partial S_i|$ is the vertex separation of $L$, and the\n194\tlast step ($i = n$, $\\partial C = \\emptyset$) costs 1. Hence\n195\t$\\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) = \\mathrm{vs}(L) + 1$. Minimising over $\\tau$ gives\n196\t$\\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1$ by (E1). The MOSP form is (E2).\n197\t$\\square$ (The Lean statement counts the active suffix of $\\tau$ itself, the\n198\tdevelopment's convention, so no reversal appears there.) (Lean: `orderCost_ofFn_eq_outNarrowness`,\n199\t`orderCost_ofFn_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_add_one`,\n200\t`searchSol_empty_iff_narrowness_le`,\n201\t`searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le`,\n202\t`searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le`; behind them\n203\t`Complex.narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` and\n204\t`mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.)\n205\t\n206\tSo the only thing a refutation, $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$, may mean is\n207\t$Z(M) > k$, equivalently $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$. The rest of this section\n208\tshows that the pruning rules do not change the answer.\n209\t\n210\t*In plain English.* Close the customers one at a time. When a customer\n211\tcloses, every neighbour must already have its stack open, so the stacks open\n212\tat that moment are the customer itself plus every not-yet-closed customer\n213\ttouching the closed group. Read the closing order backwards and this is the\n214\tcount that vertex separation takes at each cut. So the best closing order uses\n215\texactly vertex separation plus one stacks, which is pathwidth plus one.\n216\t\n217\t**A modelling remark.** The code drops customers with no product. In $G_M$\n218\tthey are isolated vertices, which are free at the root and change nothing.\n219\tThis is argued, not formalised.\n220\t\n221\t---\n222\t\n223\t## 4.3 The rules\n224\t\n225\t### 4.3.0 What a rule must satisfy\n226\t\n227\tAt a node the search has a state $S$ with the invariant, and a set $Q$ of\n228\t*old moves* (section 4.3.5). Its *candidates* are the customers outside\n229\t$S \\cup Q$. The *cost cut* keeps the playable candidates $P$, in increasing\n230\tcustomer index. The cost cut is exact, since an unplayable move is not a move\n231\tof $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. A *filter* then keeps a sublist $L \\subseteq P$, and the\n232\tsearch recurses on $S \\cdot c$ for $c \\in L$.\n233\t\n234\t**Node soundness.** Suppose $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$ for every\n235\t$q \\in Q$. The filter is node-sound at $(S, Q)$ if $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ implies\n236\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$ for some $c \\in L$ (Lean: `NodeSoundAt`;\n237\t`FilterSound` when it holds at every node with the invariant).\n238\t\n239\tEach rule is justified by a *covering*: a discarded move $r$ is covered by $c$\n240\twhen $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$. A node\n241\tis sound when every link a rule cites is a true covering and every chain of\n242\tcited links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$ (Lean:\n243\t`nodeSoundAt_of_covering`, with `Covered` for the chains). The second\n244\tcondition can fail even when every link is true, through a cycle. Section\n245\t4.3.4 has the example.\n246\t\n247\tThe rules run in the code's order: the free move on entry; the memo; the\n248\told-move candidate set; the cost cut; then the *definite move*, the *subset\n249\trule* and the *better move*, each once.\n250\t\n251\t### 4.3.1 The free move\n252\t\n253\t**Published form.** Chu & Stuckey have no separate rule. It is the\n254\t$\\mathrm{open} = 0$ case of their Theorem 1.\n255\t\n256\t**Precise form.** On entering a node at $T$, move to $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n257\t\n258\t**Soundness.** Lemma 4.2. The search's predicate is defined over states, and\n259\tby Lemma 4.2 it agrees with the plain question $P_k$ at every node the search\n260\tvisits.\n261\t\n262\t### 4.3.2 The definite move\n263\t\n264\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 1.** Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and\n265\tclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a\n266\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.1, PDF p. 6). Here close(q, S) =\n267\t|{d | o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)}| (PDF p. 4). Chu (2011) restates it word for word,\n268\twith the same proof, as Theorem 6.3.6 (p. 142), with the same definition of\n269\tclose (Definition 6.3.4, p. 141).\n270\t\n271\t**Precise form, as coded.** At a state $S$, let $q \\in P$ and let\n272\t$$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |\\{d \\in C \\setminus S : o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)\\}|,$$\n273\twhich counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\n274\tpaper's $d$ ranges over all customers. A closed $d$ has $o(d, S) = \\emptyset$\n275\tand would always be counted, so the code's reading, $d \\notin S$, is the\n276\tsensible one.) If $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$, the filter\n277\tkeeps $L = [q]$ for the first such $q$ in index order, and nothing else runs at\n278\tthe node (Lean: `closeCount`, `IsDefinite`). This was the code's rule until\n279\t2026-10-01 and is `repaired_rules=False` since; section 4.6.1 gives the rule the\n280\tcode now runs.\n281\t\n282\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n283\t\n284\tWrite $X = S \\cdot q = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ throughout.\n285\t\n286\t**Lemma 4.4 (what the premise says).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n287\t$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |X \\setminus S|$. Hence the premise\n288\t$\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$ holds iff $b(X) \\le b(S)$: the\n289\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\n290\t\n291\t*Proof.* For $d \\notin S$: $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$ iff\n292\t$N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup N[q] = O(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ iff $d \\in X$. For the second\n293\tclaim, $O(X) = O(S) \\cup N[q]$ gives\n294\t$b(X) = |O(S)| + \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - |X|$, while $b(S) = |O(S)| - |S|$, and\n295\t$|X| - |S| = \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `closeCount_eq`,\n296\t`isDefinite_iff`.)\n297\t\n298\t**Counterexample 4.5.** Let $G$ be the graph on customers $0, \\dots, 13$ with\n299\tthe 26 edges\n300\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n301\t&0\\text{–}3,\\ 0\\text{–}4,\\ 0\\text{–}7,\\ 0\\text{–}11,\\ 1\\text{–}2,\\ 1\\text{–}5,\\ 1\\text{–}6,\\ 2\\text{–}3,\\ 2\\text{–}4,\\ 5\\text{–}9,\\ 5\\text{–}11,\\ 5\\text{–}12,\\ 6\\text{–}8,\\\\\n302\t&6\\text{–}13,\\ 7\\text{–}9,\\ 7\\text{–}10,\\ 7\\text{–}13,\\ 8\\text{–}9,\\ 8\\text{–}10,\\ 8\\text{–}11,\\ 9\\text{–}10,\\ 9\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}12,\\ 10\\text{–}13,\\ 12\\text{–}13,\n303\t\\end{aligned}$$\n304\tand take $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 6$, $q = 0$. Then:\n305\t\n306\t- $S$ is a state, $O(S) = N[2] = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $b(S) = 3 \\le 6$;\n307\t- $o(0, S) = \\{0, 7, 11\\}$, so $\\mathrm{open}(0, S) = 3$, and\n308\t  $\\mathrm{cost}(S, 0) = |\\{0, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11\\}| = 6$, so $0$ is playable;\n309\t- the customers $d \\notin S$ with $o(d, S) \\subseteq \\{0, 7, 11\\}$ are $0$,\n310\t  $3$ and $4$ (both of the latter have $o = \\{0\\}$), so\n311\t  $\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge \\mathrm{open}(0, S)$;\n312\t- $0$ is the first customer in index order, so the code keeps $\\{0\\}$ alone;\n313\t- $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(S)$ holds: the order $1, 3, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$\n314\t  costs at most 6;\n315\t- the child is $X = \\{0, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(X)$ fails: only seven\n316\t  sets are reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none of them is\n317\t  $C$.\n318\t\n319\t![The counterexample to Theorem 1](figures/definite_counterexample.png)\n320\t\n321\t*Figure 4.1. The graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the state where the definite\n322\tmove fails. Customer 2 is closed, and 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens the\n323\tnew stacks 0, 7 and 11, and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the rule\n324\tkeeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them: closing\n325\tthem first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution starting\n326\twith 0 cannot match. Regenerate with `python -m paper2.counterexample_figure`,\n327\twhich recomputes every labelled fact from the instance (PDF beside it).*\n328\t\n329\tSo Theorem 1 is false, both as published and as coded. (Lean:\n330\t`definiteMove_counterexample`, `cexGraph`, `cex_solvable`, and\n331\t`cexFamily_closed` for the seven sets, checked by `decide +kernel`.) The published implication itself is refuted as a universal statement, with\n332\tthe bridge from the free-closed child to $S \\cup \\{q\\}$ inside the proof,\n333\tunder the code's reading of $\\mathrm{close}$ and under the literal one, which\n334\talso counts closed customers and so is a weaker premise\n335\t(`chuStuckey_theorem1_false`, `chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal`,\n336\t`Search/PublishedTheorems.lean`). The same two theorems refute the thesis's\n337\tTheorem 6.3.6, restated under its number as `chuThesis_theorem636_false` and\n338\t`chuThesis_theorem636_false_literal` (`Search/ChuThesis.lean`). Fink (2012,\n339\tpp. 27–28) restates the rule with a stronger premise: it counts only dominated\n340\tcustomers of smaller index than $q$, so not $q$ itself, and needs the child to\n341\thave strictly fewer open stacks than the parent. The graph of Counterexample 4.5\n342\tdoes not refute it under any labelling, but adding a third twin of $3$ and $4$\n343\tgives a 15-customer graph that refutes it (Lean: `fink_theorem1_false`;\n344\t`python3 -m paper2.fink_check`).\n345\t\n346\t**Why the published proof fails.** The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n347\tsolution $U' = S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [c_1, \\dots, c_m, q, \\dots]$ and claims that\n348\tafter each prefix \"we have at most open(q, S) extra stacks open, but at least\n349\tclose(q, S) extra stacks closed\" (PDF p. 6). Compare the two runs at a prefix\n350\t$T \\supseteq S$ of $U'$ that does not yet contain $q$. Moving $q$ forward\n351\tchanges the open stacks from $b(T)$ to $b(T \\cup X)$, and\n352\t$$b(T \\cup X) - b(T) = |N[q] \\setminus O(T)| - |X \\setminus T|.$$\n353\tAt $T = S$ this is $\\mathrm{open} - \\mathrm{close} \\le 0$. But the customers of\n354\t$X$ that $U'$ has *already* closed are in $T$, so they are no longer extra\n355\tstacks closed, while the stacks $q$ opens may still be new. In the example,\n356\t$U'$ may begin with $3$, after which $4$ is free and $T = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$. Then\n357\t$N[0] \\setminus O(T) = \\{7, 11\\}$ and $X \\setminus T = \\{0\\}$, so moving $0$\n358\tforward costs one more stack: $b(T) = 2$ and $b(T \\cup X) = 3$. Customers $3$\n359\tand $4$ each counted once in $\\mathrm{close}(0, S)$, but they share the single\n360\tnew stack $0$, and a solution that closes them first gains two closed stacks\n361\tfor one opened.\n362\t\n363\tThere is a second, independent slip. Even where the conclusion holds, the\n364\tliteral sequence $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q, c_1, \\dots]$ need not be a solution,\n365\tbecause a customer that $q$ makes free stays open until its turn comes. With\n366\tedges $0\\text{–}3$ and $1\\text{–}2$, $S = \\emptyset$ and $q = 0$\n367\t($\\mathrm{open} = \\mathrm{close} = 2$), the order $1, 2, 0, 3$ costs 2 but\n368\t$0, 1, 2, 3$ costs 3. Closing $3$ right after $0$ fixes it. The argument needs\n369\tthe free moves.\n370\t\n371\t**Repair.** Call $q \\notin S$ *hereditarily definite* at $S$ when\n372\t$$b(X) \\le b(B) \\quad \\text{for every } B \\text{ with } S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X \\text{ and } q \\notin B.$$\n373\tBy Lemma 4.4, the code's premise is the case $B = S$. The repair asks that no\n374\tintermediate set avoiding $q$ is a better place to stand than $X$ (Lean:\n375\t`IsHereditarilyDefinite`; `IsHereditarilyDefinite.isDefinite`). In the\n376\tcounterexample it fails at $B = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$, where $b(B) = 2 < 3 = b(X)$\n377\t(Lean: `not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex`).\n378\t\n379\t**Lemma 4.6 (open stacks are submodular).** For all $A, B \\subseteq C$,\n380\t$$b(A \\cup B) + b(A \\cap B) \\le b(A) + b(B).$$\n381\t\n382\t*Proof.* Since $T \\subseteq O(T)$, $b(T) = |O(T)| - |T|$. Now\n383\t$O(A \\cup B) = O(A) \\cup O(B)$ and $O(A \\cap B) \\subseteq O(A) \\cap O(B)$, so\n384\t$|O(A \\cup B)| + |O(A \\cap B)| \\le |O(A)| + |O(B)|$. Subtract\n385\t$|A \\cup B| + |A \\cap B| = |A| + |B|$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n386\t`openStacks_submodular`, from `openStacks_add_card`.)\n387\t\n388\t*In plain English.* Count the stacks opened by two groups of closed customers.\n389\tThe union of the groups opens exactly what either opens, and their overlap\n390\topens at most what both open. So the two counts on the left can only fall\n391\tshort of the two on the right, and subtracting the customers themselves, who\n392\tbalance exactly, keeps that.\n393\t\n394\t**Theorem 4.7 (the repaired definite move is sound).** If $q \\notin S$ is\n395\thereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(X)$, with no other\n396\thypothesis. At a state with the invariant,\n397\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, so keeping only a\n398\tplayable hereditarily definite $q$ is sound.\n399\t\n400\t*Proof.* Take a closing order $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ from $S$ of cost at most $k$,\n401\twith prefixes $T_i = S \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$, and let $c_j = q$. Build an\n402\torder from $X$ by following $c_1, \\dots, c_{j-1}$ and skipping any customer\n403\talready in $X$. For $i < j$ with $c_i \\notin X$, the step closes $c_i$ at\n404\t$T_{i-1} \\cup X$ and costs $b(T_i \\cup X) + 1$. Put $Z = T_i$. Then\n405\t$S \\subseteq Z \\cap X \\subseteq X$ and $q \\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n406\t$b(X) \\le b(Z \\cap X)$, and Lemma 4.6 gives\n407\t$$b(Z \\cup X) \\le b(Z) + b(X) - b(Z \\cap X) \\le b(Z).$$\n408\tSo the step costs at most $b(T_i) + 1 = \\mathrm{cost}(T_{i-1}, c_i) \\le k$.\n409\tAfter these steps the closed set is $T_{j-1} \\cup X$. It contains $T_j$, and\n410\tsince $S \\subseteq T_{j-1}$ its opened set is $O(T_{j-1}) \\cup N[q] = O(T_j)$.\n411\tThe rest of the original order gives $P_k(T_j)$, so Lemma 4.1 gives\n412\t$P_k(T_{j-1} \\cup X)$, and together $P_k(X)$.\n413\t\n414\tFor the search's form, the invariant and Lemma 4.2 give $P_k(S)$, hence\n415\t$P_k(X)$, hence $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(X)) = \\mathrm{Sol}_k(X)$ by Lemma 4.2\n416\tagain. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`, by\n417\t`solvable_union_cl_of_hereditarilyDefinite` and `stepCost_union_cl_le`;\n418\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`.)\n419\t\n420\t*In plain English.* Take any good closing order and imagine closing $q$, with\n421\teverything it makes free, before anything else. At each moment before the\n422\torder reaches $q$, compare the two situations. The hereditary condition says\n423\tthat standing on the \"$q$ and its free customers\" side never leaves more stacks\n424\topen than standing at any point between where you started and there, provided\n425\t$q$ itself has not closed. Submodularity turns that into a step-by-step\n426\tguarantee: the order with $q$ first never has more stacks open than the\n427\toriginal. Once the original order closes $q$, the two have the same stacks\n428\topen and the shifted one has closed at least as much.\n429\t\n430\t**Corollary.** Every $q$ with $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le 1$ is hereditarily\n431\tdefinite, so the code's rule is right in those cases. For\n432\t$S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X$ with $q \\notin B$, the stacks in $O(X)$ but not in\n433\t$O(B)$ lie in $N[q] \\setminus O(S) = o(q, S)$, so there is at most one of\n434\tthem. Since $O(B) \\subseteq O(X)$, $b(X) - b(B) = |O(X) \\setminus O(B)| -\n435\t|X \\setminus B| \\le 1 - 1 = 0$, because $q \\in X \\setminus B$. (Lean: `isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one`.)\n436\t\n437\t**The matching form.** The repair quantifies over all sets $B$. An equivalent\n438\tform is cheap. Let $Y = X \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$, the customers the child\n439\tcloses besides $q$, and for $D \\subseteq Y$ let\n440\t$o(D) = \\bigcup_{d \\in D} o(d, S)$. Every $d \\in Y$ has\n441\t$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$, so consider the bipartite graph joining each\n442\t$d \\in Y$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n443\t\n444\t**Theorem 4.8 (the repair is a matching condition).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n445\t$q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff some set $M \\subseteq Y$ with\n446\t$|M| \\ge \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ can be matched injectively to stacks\n447\t$f(d) \\in o(d, S)$. Equivalently, the largest matching of the bipartite graph\n448\thas at least $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ edges.\n449\t\n450\t*Proof.* Let $q \\notin S$; if $q \\in S$ then $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) = 0$ and both\n451\tsides hold trivially. The sets $B$ of the repair are exactly $B = S \\cup D$\n452\twith $D \\subseteq Y$. Since $|O(S \\cup D)| = |O(S)| + |o(D)|$ and\n453\t$|X| = |S| + 1 + |Y|$,\n454\t$$b(X) \\le b(S \\cup D) \\iff \\mathrm{open}(q, S) + |D| \\le |o(D)| + |Y| + 1 \\iff |D| - |o(D)| \\le \\delta,$$\n455\twith $\\delta = |Y| + 1 - \\mathrm{open}(q, S)$. (At $D = \\emptyset$ this gives\n456\t$\\delta \\ge 0$.) By the deficiency form of Hall's theorem (Ore 1955), the\n457\tlargest matching of $Y$ into the stacks has size\n458\t$|Y| - \\max_{D \\subseteq Y} (|D| - |o(D)|)$. So the repair holds iff that\n459\tmaximum is at most $\\delta$, iff the largest matching has size at least\n460\t$|Y| - \\delta = \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$. $\\square$\n461\t\n462\tThe Lean proof uses the textbook reduction of the deficiency form to Hall's\n463\ttheorem: give every $d \\in Y$ the same $\\delta$ dummy stacks besides $o(d, S)$,\n464\tcheck Hall's condition from the deficiency inequality, apply Mathlib's\n465\t`Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_existsInjective'`, and keep the customers\n466\tmatched to real stacks, of which at most $\\delta$ are lost. (Lean:\n467\t`HasDefiniteMatching`, `card_opened_union_eq`, `card_cl_insert`,\n468\t`hall_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`, `exists_matching_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`,\n469\t`isHereditarilyDefinite_of_matching`, and\n470\t`isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`.)\n471\t\n472\t*In plain English.* Closing $q$ opens some new stacks, and it lets some other\n473\tcustomers close for free, each of whom needed one or more of those new stacks.\n474\tThe rule is safe exactly when you can pair all but one of the new stacks with\n475\ta different free customer who needed it. In the counterexample, closing $0$\n476\topens three new stacks, $0$, $7$ and $11$, and frees customers $3$ and $4$.\n477\tBoth of them needed only stack $0$, so only one pairing is possible where two\n478\tare required.\n479\t\n480\t### 4.3.3 The subset rule\n481\t\n482\t**Published form.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can\n483\talways play i before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will\n484\tclose i in any case. Hence move j can be removed from the candidates R\n485\tconsidered for the next customer to close\" (§2, PDF p. 5).\n486\t\n487\t**Precise form, as coded.** Say $d$ *dominates* $r$ at $S$ when $d \\ne r$,\n488\t$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, and either the inclusion is strict or $d < r$.\n489\tThe rule drops every $r \\in P$ dominated by some $d \\in C \\setminus S$, where\n490\t$d$ ranges over all remaining customers, including those in $Q$. If every\n491\tcandidate would be dropped, it keeps $P$ whole (the *fallback*). It runs only\n492\twhen the definite move did not fire (Lean: `Dominates`, `IsSubsetDominated`,\n493\t`subsetFilter`).\n494\t\n495\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$\n496\tfor the dominator $d$. **Sound as coded.**\n497\t\n498\t**Lemma 4.9 (the covering).** If $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$ and\n499\t$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, then\n500\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$, for every set\n501\t$S$, with no invariant and no tie-break. Moreover $d$ is playable.\n502\t\n503\t*Proof.* $N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup o(d, S) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, so\n504\t$d \\in S \\cdot r$, and $O(S \\cup \\{d\\}) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ gives\n505\t$S \\cdot d \\subseteq S \\cdot r$ and $\\mathrm{cost}(S, d) \\le \\mathrm{cost}(S, r)$.\n506\tIf $r \\in S \\cdot d$, the two opened sets are equal and $S \\cdot d = S \\cdot r$.\n507\tOtherwise close $r$ at $S \\cdot d$. It costs\n508\t$|O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot d| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S| =\n509\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, and it lands on\n510\t$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S \\cup \\{r\\})) = S \\cdot r$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n511\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`, with\n512\t`mem_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n513\t`cl_insert_subset_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n514\t`cl_insert_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n515\t`stepCost_le_of_newlyOpened_subset`.)\n516\t\n517\t**Proposition 4.10 (node soundness).** At any state $S \\ne C$, if every\n518\t$q \\in Q$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, the subset filter is node-sound.\n519\tWhen a solution exists, the fallback never fires.\n520\t\n521\t*Proof.* Domination is irreflexive and transitive, so it is a strict order.\n522\tGiven $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, some playable $r$ has $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r)$,\n523\tand $r \\notin Q$. Among the customers $m \\notin S$ with\n524\t$o(m, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, take the least by $(|o(m, S)|, m)$. Any dominator\n525\tof $m$ would come earlier in that order, so $m$ is undominated. By Lemma 4.9,\n526\t$m$ is playable and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot m)$ holds, so $m \\notin Q$. So $m$\n527\tis a candidate that survives. $\\square$ (Lean: `not_dominates_self`,\n528\t`Dominates.trans`, `exists_undominated`, `subsetKept_nonempty`,\n529\t`subsetFilter_sound`.)\n530\t\n531\t*In plain English.* If closing $d$ opens only stacks that closing $r$ would\n532\topen anyway, then closing $r$ makes $d$ free. So closing $d$ first, then $r$,\n533\treaches the same place as closing $r$, never at a higher cost, and the search\n534\tmay as well try $d$. Follow such domination downwards and you reach a customer\n535\tnobody dominates, which the rule keeps.\n536\t\n537\t**The tie-break is necessary (Counterexample 4.11).** Without it, two\n538\tcustomers with equal new stacks dominate each other and both go. Take the\n539\t7-customer graph with edges $0\\text{–}2$, $0\\text{–}3$, $0\\text{–}6$,\n540\t$1\\text{–}4$, $1\\text{–}6$, $3\\text{–}6$, $4\\text{–}5$, $4\\text{–}6$, the\n541\tstate $S = \\{2\\}$ and $k = 3$. The playable candidates are $\\{0, 3, 5\\}$, with\n542\t$o(0, S) = o(3, S) = \\{3, 6\\}$ and $o(5, S) = \\{4, 5\\}$. Without the tie-break\n543\t$0$ and $3$ both go, $5$ survives, so the fallback does not fire, and the\n544\tfilter keeps $\\{5\\}$. A solution exists from $S$ ($0, 3, 1, 4, 5, 6$), but none\n545\tfrom $S \\cdot 5 = \\{2, 5\\}$, where no move costs at most 3. With the tie-break,\n546\tonly $3$ goes and the filter keeps $\\{0, 5\\}$. (Lean:\n547\t`noTieBreak_counterexample`, `tieGraph`, `WeakDominates`, `weakSubsetFilter`.)\n548\tThe paper's own form, which requires $d < r$ even for a strict inclusion, is a\n549\tsubrelation of the code's, uses the same covering and is acyclic by index. It\n550\tis checked, not stated in Lean.\n551\t\n552\t### 4.3.4 The better move\n553\t\n554\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 2.** Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are\n555\tplayable and close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is\n556\ta solution there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.2, PDF p. 6). The\n557\tproof begins: \"The conditions imply that if r is played now, q becomes a\n558\tdefinite move.\"\n559\t\n560\t**Precise form, as coded** (the published premises; in the C since 2026-09-26, in\n561\tthe Python since 2026-10-01, and under `repaired_rules=False` since then). The input is the\n562\tlist $W$ of subset survivors, in index order. Let $X_r = O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. A\n563\tmember $r$ is dropped if some earlier $q \\in W$, among the first $L$ of $W$ if\n564\t$L > 0$, satisfies\n565\t\n566\t- *(premise 3)* $|(X_r \\cup N[q]) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k$, and\n567\t- *(premise 4)* $\\mathrm{open}' \\le \\mathrm{close}'$, where\n568\t  $\\mathrm{open}' = |N[q] \\setminus X_r|$ and $\\mathrm{close}'$ counts the\n569\t  $d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus X_r \\subseteq N[q] \\setminus X_r$.\n570\t\n571\tPremise 2 is $q \\in W \\subseteq P$. Whether $q$ itself survives is not\n572\tconsulted. (Lean: `IsBetter`, `betterCite`, `withinLimit`, `betterFilterBy`.)\n573\t\n574\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n575\t\n576\t**Lemma 4.12 (the premise is Theorem 1 at the child).** Premises 3 and 4 hold\n577\tiff $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q) \\le k$ and the code's definite premise\n578\tholds for $q$ at the child $T = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$.\n579\t\n580\t*Proof.* Premise 3 is $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q)$ by definition. Since\n581\t$O(T) = X_r$, $\\mathrm{open}' = \\mathrm{open}(q, T)$. A customer\n582\t$d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ has $N[d] \\setminus X_r \\ne \\emptyset$ exactly when\n583\t$d \\notin T$, and then $N[d] \\setminus X_r = o(d, T)$. So\n584\t$\\mathrm{close}' = \\mathrm{close}(q, T)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `isBetter_iff`,\n585\t`betterOpen_eq`, `betterClose_eq`.)\n586\t\n587\tSo the better move is Theorem 1 at the child, followed by a swap of $q$ and\n588\t$r$. The swap is sound. The appeal to Theorem 1 is not.\n589\t\n590\t**Counterexample 4.13.** Take the graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the root,\n591\t$S = \\emptyset$, with $k = 6$ and $Q = \\emptyset$. No playable customer meets\n592\tthe definite premise, so the definite move does not fire. Customers $0$ and $2$\n593\tboth survive the subset rule, $0 < 2$, and premises 3 and 4 hold for $r = 2$,\n594\t$q = 0$, so the code drops $2$ citing $0$. But $S \\cdot 2 = \\{2\\}$ has a\n595\tsolution (Counterexample 4.5), and $S \\cdot 0 = \\{0\\}$ has none. The child\n596\t$\\{2\\}$ is exactly the state at which Theorem 1 wrongly moves $0$ forward.\n597\t(Lean: `betterMove_counterexample`, by an invariant family of 18 sets,\n598\t`bmFamily_closed`.) Theorem 2's published implication is refuted the same way, under both\n599\treadings of $\\mathrm{close}$ (`chuStuckey_theorem2_false`,\n600\t`chuStuckey_theorem2_false_literal`). At this node the filter still keeps customer $1$, which has\n601\ta solution, so this is a *false link*, not a lost node (Lean:\n602\t`betterMove_counterexample_node`). No node where the better move loses the last\n603\tsolution has been found (section 4.5).\n604\t\n605\t**The thesis's form.** Chu (2011) states the better move as \"**Theorem 6.3.8.**\n606\tSuppose S is some sequence, and q, r ∉ S are customers such that: S ++ [q] and\n607\tS ++ [r, q] are both k-playable, and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}). If\n608\tS ++ [r] has an extension that uses ≤ k stacks, then S ++ [q] also has an\n609\textension that uses ≤ k stacks\" (p. 143). The premise takes close at $S$, not at\n610\t$S \\cup \\{r\\}$. The proof derives the CP premise from it\n611\t(\"close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}), so after r is played, q\n612\tbecomes a definite move\") and then appeals to Theorem 6.3.6, so it fails at the\n613\tsame step. Counterexample 4.13 does not cover it, but the graph of\n614\tCounterexample 4.5 does: take $S = \\{2\\}$, $r = 3$, $q = 0$, $k = 6$. Then\n615\t$\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge 2 = \\mathrm{open}(0, S \\cup \\{3\\})$, both\n616\t$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3, 0]$ are playable (costs 6\n617\tand 5), $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3]$ extends by\n618\t$1, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$, and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ has no\n619\textension, which is Counterexample 4.5's child. Under the literal reading of\n620\tclose the premise is weaker and the same witness serves; a second witness,\n621\t$S = \\{1\\}$, $r = 6$, $q = 12$, holds under the literal reading only.\n622\t(Lean: `chuThesis_theorem638_false`, `chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal`,\n623\t`chuThesis_theorem638_literal_witness`, `Search/ChuThesis.lean`; the brute\n624\tforce over all $2^{14}$ sets that found the witnesses is\n625\t`python3 paper2/thesis_check.py`: 13 failing (state, $r$, $q$) triples under\n626\tthe code's reading, 22 under the literal one.)\n627\t\n628\t**Repair.** Cite $q$ for $r$ only if premise 3 holds and $q$ is hereditarily\n629\tdefinite at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ (Lean: `IsRepairedBetter`;\n630\t`IsRepairedBetter.isBetter` shows it implies the code's premise). By Theorem\n631\t4.8 this is one matching at the child.\n632\t\n633\t**Theorem 4.14 (the repaired better move is sound).** If $r, q \\notin S$,\n634\t$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$ and the repaired premise holds, then\n635\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n636\t\n637\t*Proof.* Let $T = S \\cdot r$, which has the invariant because $r$ is playable.\n638\tIf $q \\in T$, put $T' = T$. Otherwise Theorem 4.7 at $T$ gives\n639\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(T')$ for $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T \\cup \\{q\\})$. Now let\n640\t$Z = S \\cdot q$. Both $\\mathrm{cl}(Z \\cup \\{r\\})$ and $T'$ equal\n641\t$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[r])$. If $r \\in Z$ then $Z = T'$. Otherwise\n642\tclosing $r$ at $Z$ costs\n643\t$$|O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus Z| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| = |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k,$$\n644\twhere the middle equality exchanges $q$ and $r$, both of which are opened and\n645\tneither of which is in $S$, and the last step is premise 3. So $r$ is a\n646\tplayable move from $Z$ to $T'$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(Z)$ follows. $\\square$\n647\t(Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`,\n648\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`, with `card_sdiff_insert_eq`.) The\n649\timplication needs $r$ playable, not $q$. Playability of $q$ is what makes $q$ a\n650\tchild of the search.\n651\t\n652\t*In plain English.* The better move says: \"if closing $r$ now would make $q$ a\n653\tsafe next move, then closing $q$ first and $r$ second is at least as good as\n654\tclosing $r$ first.\" The swap at the end is fine, because both orders end in the\n655\tsame place and neither step overflows. What fails is \"would make $q$ a safe\n656\tnext move\", which was Theorem 1. With the repaired test there, the argument\n657\tgoes through unchanged.\n658\t\n659\t**Two earlier faults, now proved.** The 2026-09-26 fix corrected two bugs in\n660\tthis rule, both caught by testing (`../reports/better_move_bug.md` §7). Lean\n661\tnow proves a counterexample to each.\n662\t\n663\t- *Bug A, the close count.* Counting also the customers that $r$ finishes on\n664\t  its own makes the rule's own conclusion false, on a 12-customer graph at\n665\t  $k = 4$ (Lean: `IsBetterOld`, `bugA_counterexample`, `bugAGraph`).\n666\t- *Bug B, the order of the rules.* Running the better move before the subset\n667\t  rule lets the subset rule cite a customer the better move has already\n668\t  dropped. On an 8-customer graph at $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 4$, the better move drops\n669\t  $3$ citing $0$ and the subset rule drops $0$ citing $3$. Every premise is the\n670\t  corrected one and true, and the node is lost through the cycle (Lean:\n671\t  `oldOrderFilter`, `bugB_counterexample`, `bugBGraph`).\n672\t\n673\t### 4.3.5 The old move\n674\t\n675\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 3.** Let S = [s₁, s₂, …, sₙ]. Suppose that\n676\tS′ = [s₁, s₂, .., sₘ, q, sₘ₊₁, …, sₙ] is playable, then if U′ = S ++ [q] ++ R is\n677\ta solution then U = S′ ++ R is a solution\" (§3.3, PDF p. 7), with the rule\n678\t\"if it is found that at some ancestor node, the q branch has been searched and\n679\tU is playable, then q can immediately be pruned\".\n680\t\n681\t**Precise form, as coded.** A node carries a set $Q$ of old moves, empty at the\n682\troot. When a candidate's subtree returns *false* without aborting, the\n683\tcandidate joins the node's list `seen`. A later sibling $c'$ passes to its child\n684\tthose $q \\in$ `seen` with\n685\t$$\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c') = |(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c']) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k,$$\n686\twhich is the test that the step $c'$ stays playable with $q$ reinserted before\n687\tit. Old moves are removed from the candidates (Lean: `Inherits`, `pathEnd`).\n688\t\n689\t**Claimed soundness.** Every $q \\in Q$ at a node has\n690\t$\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. **Sound as coded.**\n691\t\n692\t**Lemma 4.15 (reinsertion).** If $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c) \\le k$, then\n693\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\n694\tfor every set $S$, with no invariant. Iterated along a path whose every move\n695\tpasses the test: if the $q$ branch at an ancestor has no solution, neither has\n696\t$q$ at the end of the path.\n697\t\n698\t*Proof.* Both $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)$ and\n699\t$\\mathrm{cl}(\\{c\\} \\cup S \\cdot q)$ equal $\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c])$.\n700\tIf $c \\in S \\cdot q$, then the second is $S \\cdot q$ itself. Otherwise $c$ is a\n701\tmove at $S \\cdot q$ of cost\n702\t$|O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot q| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k$,\n703\tbecause $S \\cup \\{q\\} \\subseteq S \\cdot q$. Induction on the path gives the\n704\tsecond statement. $\\square$ (Lean: `searchSol_reinsert`,\n705\t`cl_insert_cl_insert_comm`, `stepCost_cl_le`, `searchSol_reinsert_path`,\n706\t`not_searchSol_of_oldMove`.)\n707\t\n708\t**The test is necessary.** On the path $4\\text{–}0\\text{–}2\\text{–}1\\text{–}3$\n709\twith $S = \\emptyset$, $q = 2$, $c = 3$ and $k = 2$, the move $c$ is playable but\n710\t$\\mathrm{cost}(\\{2\\}, 3) = 3$. Here\n711\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{2\\} \\cup \\mathrm{cl}\\{3\\})) = \\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{1, 2, 3\\})$\n712\tholds and $\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{2\\})$ does not. This is the smallest such graph,\n713\tby exhaustion to 5 vertices (Lean: `reinsert_needs_test`). Chu & Stuckey make\n714\tthe same point: the condition \"is in fact crucial\" (PDF p. 7).\n715\t\n716\t*In plain English.* Suppose that at some earlier node the search tried closing\n717\t$q$ and found no way to finish. Since then it has closed some other customers.\n718\tIf, at every one of those steps, closing $q$ first would still have kept the\n719\tstep within budget, then \"close $q$ now\" leads to a place the search could also\n720\thave reached from the earlier attempt. That attempt failed, so this one fails\n721\ttoo, and $q$ can be skipped.\n722\t\n723\tThe paper's \"synergy\" (adding $r$ to $Q$ when an old move is better than $r$)\n724\tis implemented in neither the C nor the Python and is not covered.\n725\t\n726\t### 4.3.6 The memo\n727\t\n728\t**Published form.** Nogood recording: \"if it failed, we record the nogood and\n729\treturn false when we revisit it\" (PDF p. 4).\n730\t\n731\t**Precise form, as coded.** When a node's loop finishes with no child answering\n732\t*true* and without an abort, its closed set $S$ is recorded. At any later node\n733\twith the same closed set the search answers *false* at once. The memo may\n734\tstore fewer entries (a limit, a load factor), which only stores less.\n735\t\n736\t**Claimed soundness.** A recorded $S$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n737\t\n738\t**Lemma 4.16 (the key).** If $b(T) \\le k$, $b(T') \\le k$ and\n739\t$\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{cl}(T')$, then $P_k(T) \\iff P_k(T')$. So a state\n740\trefuted once is refuted whatever path reached it.\n741\t\n742\t*Proof.* Lemma 4.2 at both sides. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_of_cl_eq`.)\n743\t\n744\tThe memo is sound once every recorded entry is a genuine refutation. With old\n745\tmove on, that is not a property of the node alone, and it is proved for the\n746\twhole run in section 4.4.2.\n747\t\n748\t---\n749\t\n750\t## 4.4 The composition and the theorem\n751\t\n752\t### 4.4.1 The filter\n753\t\n754\tThe filter runs in the order\n755\t\n756\t$$\\text{definite move} \\;\\to\\; \\text{subset rule} \\;\\to\\; \\text{better move},$$\n757\t\n758\teach once, and each cites only customers still standing or in $Q$. If the\n759\tdefinite move fires, $L = [q]$. Otherwise the subset rule runs over $P$ with\n760\tdominators from all of $C \\setminus S$, while nothing has yet been discarded.\n761\tThe better move then runs over the subset survivors and cites only an earlier\n762\tsurvivor (Lean: `fullFilter`; `codeFullFilter` with the code's premises,\n763\t`repairedFullFilter` with both repairs).\n764\t\n765\t**Proposition 4.17 (the repaired filter is node-sound).** At every state with\n766\tthe invariant, for every limit $L$ and every set $Q$ of refuted old moves,\n767\t$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$ with both repairs is\n768\tnode-sound.\n769\t\n770\t*Proof.* If the definite move fires on a playable hereditarily definite $q$,\n771\tTheorem 4.7 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Otherwise Proposition 4.10 gives\n772\ta subset survivor with a solution. Let $w$ be the earliest such survivor in\n773\tindex order. If the better move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor $q$\n774\tunder the repaired premise, and Theorem 4.14 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\n775\twhich contradicts the choice of $w$. So $w \\in L$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n776\t`betterFilterBy_sound`, `fullFilter_sound`, `repairedFilter_sound`,\n777\t`repairedFullFilter_sound`.)\n778\t\n779\tThe argument needs no chain of citations and does not use $L$. Bug B shows\n780\tthat the order matters. With the better move first, a subset link can point\n781\tbackwards at a customer already discarded, and every premise can be true while\n782\tthe chain cycles.\n783\t\n784\t*In plain English.* If the safe-move test fires, the one move it keeps is\n785\tproved safe. If it does not, the subset rule always keeps some move that still\n786\tleads to a solution. Among the moves that do, look at the first one. The better\n787\tmove can only throw it out by pointing at an even earlier move that it proves\n788\tat least as good, and there is none. So something that still works always\n789\tsurvives.\n790\t\n791\t### 4.4.2 Runs, the memo and old move together\n792\t\n793\tA run of the search is described by a big-step semantics of its *false*\n794\tanswers (Lean: `Exec`). A node intersects $Q$ with its remaining customers,\n795\tenumerates the filter's output in any order, runs each child with any subset of\n796\tthe old moves that pass the reinsertion test, adds each refuted child to\n797\t`seen`, and records its state in the memo or not. A memo hit answers *false*.\n798\tAn aborted run produces no `Exec` at all, which matches the code: an abort\n799\tanswers *unknown* and records nothing. So the child order, the memo policy and\n800\told move on or off are all covered.\n801\t\n802\t**Theorem 4.18 (runs are sound).** Let the filter be node-sound and return only\n803\tplayable customers. Suppose that at the start of a run the invariant holds at\n804\t$S$, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and every $q \\in Q \\setminus S$\n805\thas $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Then the run leaves only genuine memo\n806\tentries, and if it answers *false* then $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n807\t\n808\t*Proof.* Induct on the run, which follows the order in which subtrees complete.\n809\tEach entry of `seen` at a node is either a sibling subtree that has already\n810\tcompleted with *false*, refuted by the induction hypothesis, or one of the\n811\tnode's own old moves, refuted by hypothesis. Each old move a child inherits\n812\tpassed the test, so Lemma 4.15 makes it genuinely refuted at the child. When a\n813\tnode's loop has refuted every member of the filter's output, node soundness"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3e233eac-acc6-45da-bdf4-c3a7e4de0805","uuid":"d57faa98-ceac-4ff6-909b-0a78ea503852","timestamp":"2026-10-03T01:12:49.145Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/revised_algorithm.md","content":"# Chu & Stuckey's (revised) algorithm\n\nSection 4 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md` §4) in paper form. It distils\nthe working report `search_soundness.md` (Ralph loop0006, items 05 to 13),\nwhich holds the code references, the full check tables and the history. Every\nstatement here is proved in Lean unless it says otherwise. The Lean names\nrefer to `../lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`, namespace\n`MOSPFormalization.Search`: nine files (`Basic`, `DefiniteMove`,\n`SubsetRule`, `BetterMove`, `Memo`, `Decide`, `DefiniteMatching`,\n`PublishedTheorems`, `ChuThesis`), with no\n`sorry` and no axioms beyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`\n(`axiom_check.lean`). Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the\npreprint `../literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`. Page numbers for Chu (2011),\nChu's PhD thesis, which restates the search in its chapter 6, are the printed\nones of `../literature/chu_2011_phd_thesis_improving_combinatorial_optimization.pdf`.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1 Introduction\n\nLet $M$ be a MOSP instance and $k \\ge 0$ an integer. The algorithm of this\nsection decides whether some sequence of the products keeps at most $k$\ncustomer stacks open at once, that is, whether $Z(M) \\le k$. By (E2) of\nsection 3 (Lean: `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`), $Z(M) \\le k$ holds exactly\nwhen the MOSP graph $G_M$ has pathwidth at most $k - 1$. So the algorithm is\nalso a decision procedure for pathwidth. Any graph $G$ is the MOSP graph of the\ninstance with one customer per vertex and one product per edge, each product\nneeded by the two ends of its edge.\n\nThe algorithm is the *customer search* of Chu & Stuckey (2009). It searches\nover the orders in which the customers' stacks close, not over product\nsequences, and it prunes the search with several dominance rules. Chu &\nStuckey state three of these rules as theorems (their Theorems 1, 2 and 3) and\na fourth, the subset rule, in their §2.\n\n**Two of the published theorems are false as stated.** Theorem 1, the\n*definite move*, can discard the last solution at a node. A 14-customer graph\nshows it, and Lean proves the counterexample. Theorem 2, the *better move*,\nrests on Theorem 1 applied one step deeper, and it inherits the fault. Its\nform as the code ran it from 2026-09-26 to 2026-10-01 is still false. Chu's\nthesis (Chu 2011, §6.3) restates both: its Theorem 6.3.6 is Theorem 1\nverbatim, and its Theorem 6.3.8 is the better move with a different premise.\nThe same graph refutes both, in Lean.\n\n**The revised algorithm** keeps the search and every rule. It replaces the\npremises of Theorems 1 and 2 by a stronger, *hereditary* premise. That premise\nis proved sound, and it is proved equivalent to a bipartite matching\ncondition, so it costs one small matching per candidate. With the two repairs,\nthe whole search is proved sound in Lean: a refutation at $k$ means\n$Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$.\n\nNo wrong answer of the unrepaired search on a whole instance is known. The\ngap is at the level of a node, and the search has always recovered through\nanother branch.\n\n**The owner's decision** (2026-10-01, `plan.md`, *Decisions*), now carried\nout. This paper states the gap and the repaired rule. Both solvers, the MOSP\ncustomer search and the graph pathwidth solver, apply the repaired rules by\ndefault since 2026-10-01 (Ralph loop0007, `solver_fix.md`), and the published\nrules remain behind a flag. Of the 115 corpus values that rested on the\ncustomer search alone, 108 have been re-refuted by the repaired solver and none\nchanged; the other 7 need a longer run. Section 4.4.4 states what holds for the\ncode, and section 4.6.3 what is left.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 The search as a mathematical object\n\n### 4.2.1 Definitions\n\nChu & Stuckey observe that the products matter only through the MOSP graph:\n\"all functions c(p) that produce the same customer graph have the same minimum\nnumber of stacks\" (PDF p. 4). So fix a finite simple graph $G = (C, E)$ whose\nvertices are the customers, and an integer $k \\ge 0$. For a customer $c$ and a\nset $T \\subseteq C$, write\n\n- $N[c] = \\{c\\} \\cup \\{d : cd \\in E\\}$, the closed neighbourhood: the stacks\n  that must be open when $c$'s stack closes;\n- $O(T) = \\bigcup_{c \\in T} N[c]$, the stacks *opened* once the customers of\n  $T$ have closed, with $O(\\emptyset) = \\emptyset$;\n- $\\mathrm{fin}(X) = \\{c : N[c] \\subseteq X\\}$, the customers that could close\n  at no cost once the stacks $X$ are open;\n- $\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{fin}(O(T))$, the *free closure* of $T$;\n- $b(T) = |O(T) \\setminus T|$, the stacks open after $T$ has closed;\n- $o(c, T) = N[c] \\setminus O(T)$, the stacks that closing $c$ next would newly\n  open, and $\\mathrm{open}(c, T) = |o(c, T)|$.\n\nTwo facts are immediate: $T \\subseteq \\mathrm{cl}(T)$ and\n$O(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) = O(T)$ (Lean: `subset_cl`, `opened_cl`). The second says\nthat free closing opens nothing.\n\n**Moves and costs.** A *move* at $T$ closes one customer $c \\notin T$. Its cost\nis the number of stacks open at that moment, counting $c$ itself:\n$$\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) = |O(T \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus T| = b(T \\cup \\{c\\}) + 1.$$\nThis is Chu & Stuckey's $|O(S) - S \\cup o(c, S)|$ (PDF p. 4). The move is\n*playable* when $\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) \\le k$. A *closing order from* $T$ is an\nenumeration $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ of $C \\setminus T$. Its cost is the largest of\nits step costs $\\mathrm{cost}(T \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_{i-1}\\}, c_i)$, and $0$\nwhen $m = 0$. Write $P_k(T)$ when some closing order from $T$ costs at most\n$k$. (Lean: `stepCost`, `orderCost`, `Solvable`;\n`stepCost_eq_openStacks_insert_add_one` for the second form of the cost.)\n\n**States.** On entering a node, the search closes every customer whose\nneighbourhood is already open. One pass suffices, because closing such a\ncustomer opens nothing. So the search only ever stands at a *state*, a set\n$S$ with $S = \\mathrm{cl}(S)$. The root $\\emptyset$ is a state, since every\n$N[c]$ is nonempty. The *child* of a state $S$ by $c \\notin S$ is\n$$S \\cdot c = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{c\\}).$$\n\n**The decision predicate.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ holds when $S = C$, or when some\n$c \\notin S$ has $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$ and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\nThis is the question the search answers: \"is there a run of playable moves,\neach followed by its free moves, that closes everyone?\" (Lean: `SearchSol`,\ndefined inductively and not through $P_k$, so that the link between the two is\na theorem.)\n\n**The invariant.** Every node the search visits satisfies\n$$b(S) = |O(S) \\setminus S| \\le k.$$\nIt holds at the root, where $b(\\emptyset) = 0$. At a child it holds because\n$b(S \\cdot c) \\le |O(S \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{c\\})| =\n\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$ (Lean: `card_opened_insert_sdiff_lt`). Several\nresults below need it, and it is never an extra assumption about the search.\n\n### 4.2.2 Lemma F: free moves never hurt\n\n**Lemma 4.1 (monotonicity).** If $T \\subseteq T'$ and $O(T') \\subseteq O(T)$,\nthen $P_k(T) \\Rightarrow P_k(T')$.\n\n*Proof.* Take a closing order from $T$ of cost at most $k$ and delete the\ncustomers of $T'$ from it. Each remaining step closes some $c$ at a prefix\n$T' \\cup A$ in place of $T \\cup A$. Its opened set\n$O(T' \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\}) \\subseteq O(T \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\})$ is no larger, and the\nset removed from it, $T' \\cup A \\supseteq T \\cup A$, is no smaller. So no step\ncosts more. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_mono`, via `orderCost_filter_le`.)\n\n**Lemma 4.2 (Lemma F).** If $b(T) \\le k$, then\n$P_k(T) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$. The direction ($\\Rightarrow$)\nneeds no hypothesis. At the root, $P_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$.\n\n*Proof.* ($\\Rightarrow$) By Lemma 4.1 with $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T)$, it suffices\nto show $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ for a state $S$. Induct on\n$|C \\setminus S|$. If $S \\ne C$, let $c$ be the first move of an order from\n$S$ of cost at most $k$. Then $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$, the rest of the\norder gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, Lemma 4.1 gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$, and\ninduction gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n\n($\\Leftarrow$) First, $P_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) \\Rightarrow P_k(T)$ when\n$b(T) \\le k$. Close the customers of $\\mathrm{cl}(T) \\setminus T$ one at a\ntime. Each step at a prefix $U$ with $T \\subseteq U$ costs\n$|O(T) \\setminus U| \\le b(T) \\le k$, because the customer closed lies in\n$O(T)$ already. Then follow the order from $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\nSecond, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(S)$ at a state with the invariant,\nby induction on $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. At a move $c$, the child $S \\cdot c$\nsatisfies the invariant, so induction gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$. The first step\nthen gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, because $b(S \\cup \\{c\\}) =\n\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$. Prepending $c$, at cost at most $k$, gives\n$P_k(S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_searchSol_cl`,\n`searchSol_cl_of_solvable`, `solvable_of_searchSol`, `solvable_cl_iff`;\nat the root, `solvable_empty_iff`.)\n\nThe hypothesis is needed. On $K_2$ with one customer closed and $k = 0$,\n$\\mathrm{cl}(T)$ is everything, so $\\mathrm{Sol}_0(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$ holds, but\nclosing the other customer costs one stack.\n\n*In plain English.* Once every neighbour of a customer has an open stack, that\ncustomer can close straight away without opening anything new. Doing so never\nmakes later steps dearer, and it makes some cheaper, because one fewer stack\nis left open. So a search that always closes such customers first loses\nnothing. The only proviso is that the stacks already open must fit within the\nbudget, since closing the free customers one by one costs exactly that many.\n\n### 4.2.3 Cost is narrowness, so the search decides pathwidth\n\nFor a full closing order $\\tau = c_1, \\dots, c_n$ from $\\emptyset$, write\n$S_i = \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$ and\n$\\partial X = \\{v \\notin X : v \\text{ has a neighbour in } X\\}$. The $i$-th step\ncosts\n$$|O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}| = 1 + |\\partial S_i|,$$\nbecause $O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}$ is $c_i$ together with the customers\noutside $S_i$ that have a neighbour in $S_i$. The maximum of these numbers over\n$i$ is the *narrowness of the sequence* $\\tau$, in the dual shack process of\nKornai & Tuza (1992), formalised as `Complex.outNarrowness`.\n\n**Theorem 4.3 (the search decides pathwidth and MOSP).** For nonempty $C$,\n$$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\min_\\tau \\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) \\le k \\iff \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 \\le k.$$\nFor a MOSP instance $M$ with at least one requirement, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$\non $G_M$ holds iff $Z(M) \\le k$.\n\n*Proof.* The first equivalence is Lemma 4.2 at the root. For the second, read\n$\\tau$ backwards as a layout $L$, so that the cut after position $n - i$ of\n$L$ separates $C \\setminus S_i$ (left) from $S_i$ (right). The left vertices\nwith a right neighbour are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So\n$\\max_{1 \\le i < n} |\\partial S_i|$ is the vertex separation of $L$, and the\nlast step ($i = n$, $\\partial C = \\emptyset$) costs 1. Hence\n$\\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) = \\mathrm{vs}(L) + 1$. Minimising over $\\tau$ gives\n$\\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1$ by (E1). The MOSP form is (E2).\n$\\square$ (The Lean statement counts the active suffix of $\\tau$ itself, the\ndevelopment's convention, so no reversal appears there.) (Lean: `orderCost_ofFn_eq_outNarrowness`,\n`orderCost_ofFn_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_add_one`,\n`searchSol_empty_iff_narrowness_le`,\n`searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le`,\n`searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le`; behind them\n`Complex.narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` and\n`mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.)\n\nSo the only thing a refutation, $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$, may mean is\n$Z(M) > k$, equivalently $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$. The rest of this section\nshows that the pruning rules do not change the answer.\n\n*In plain English.* Close the customers one at a time. When a customer\ncloses, every neighbour must already have its stack open, so the stacks open\nat that moment are the customer itself plus every not-yet-closed customer\ntouching the closed group. Read the closing order backwards and this is the\ncount that vertex separation takes at each cut. So the best closing order uses\nexactly vertex separation plus one stacks, which is pathwidth plus one.\n\n**A modelling remark.** The code drops customers with no product. In $G_M$\nthey are isolated vertices, which are free at the root and change nothing.\nThis is argued, not formalised.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 The rules\n\n### 4.3.0 What a rule must satisfy\n\nAt a node the search has a state $S$ with the invariant, and a set $Q$ of\n*old moves* (section 4.3.5). Its *candidates* are the customers outside\n$S \\cup Q$. The *cost cut* keeps the playable candidates $P$, in increasing\ncustomer index. The cost cut is exact, since an unplayable move is not a move\nof $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. A *filter* then keeps a sublist $L \\subseteq P$, and the\nsearch recurses on $S \\cdot c$ for $c \\in L$.\n\n**Node soundness.** Suppose $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$ for every\n$q \\in Q$. The filter is node-sound at $(S, Q)$ if $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ implies\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$ for some $c \\in L$ (Lean: `NodeSoundAt`;\n`FilterSound` when it holds at every node with the invariant).\n\nEach rule is justified by a *covering*: a discarded move $r$ is covered by $c$\nwhen $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$. A node\nis sound when every link a rule cites is a true covering and every chain of\ncited links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$ (Lean:\n`nodeSoundAt_of_covering`, with `Covered` for the chains). The second\ncondition can fail even when every link is true, through a cycle. Section\n4.3.4 has the example.\n\nThe rules run in the code's order: the free move on entry; the memo; the\nold-move candidate set; the cost cut; then the *definite move*, the *subset\nrule* and the *better move*, each once.\n\n### 4.3.1 The free move\n\n**Published form.** Chu & Stuckey have no separate rule. It is the\n$\\mathrm{open} = 0$ case of their Theorem 1.\n\n**Precise form.** On entering a node at $T$, move to $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n\n**Soundness.** Lemma 4.2. The search's predicate is defined over states, and\nby Lemma 4.2 it agrees with the plain question $P_k$ at every node the search\nvisits.\n\n### 4.3.2 The definite move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 1.** Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and\nclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.1, PDF p. 6). Here close(q, S) =\n|{d | o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)}| (PDF p. 4). Chu (2011) restates it word for word,\nwith the same proof, as Theorem 6.3.6 (p. 142), with the same definition of\nclose (Definition 6.3.4, p. 141).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** At a state $S$, let $q \\in P$ and let\n$$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |\\{d \\in C \\setminus S : o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)\\}|,$$\nwhich counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\npaper's $d$ ranges over all customers. A closed $d$ has $o(d, S) = \\emptyset$\nand would always be counted, so the code's reading, $d \\notin S$, is the\nsensible one.) If $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$, the filter\nkeeps $L = [q]$ for the first such $q$ in index order, and nothing else runs at\nthe node (Lean: `closeCount`, `IsDefinite`). This was the code's rule until\n2026-10-01 and is `repaired_rules=False` since; section 4.6.1 gives the rule the\ncode now runs.\n\n**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n\nWrite $X = S \\cdot q = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ throughout.\n\n**Lemma 4.4 (what the premise says).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |X \\setminus S|$. Hence the premise\n$\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$ holds iff $b(X) \\le b(S)$: the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\n\n*Proof.* For $d \\notin S$: $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$ iff\n$N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup N[q] = O(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ iff $d \\in X$. For the second\nclaim, $O(X) = O(S) \\cup N[q]$ gives\n$b(X) = |O(S)| + \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - |X|$, while $b(S) = |O(S)| - |S|$, and\n$|X| - |S| = \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `closeCount_eq`,\n`isDefinite_iff`.)\n\n**Counterexample 4.5.** Let $G$ be the graph on customers $0, \\dots, 13$ with\nthe 26 edges\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&0\\text{–}3,\\ 0\\text{–}4,\\ 0\\text{–}7,\\ 0\\text{–}11,\\ 1\\text{–}2,\\ 1\\text{–}5,\\ 1\\text{–}6,\\ 2\\text{–}3,\\ 2\\text{–}4,\\ 5\\text{–}9,\\ 5\\text{–}11,\\ 5\\text{–}12,\\ 6\\text{–}8,\\\\\n&6\\text{–}13,\\ 7\\text{–}9,\\ 7\\text{–}10,\\ 7\\text{–}13,\\ 8\\text{–}9,\\ 8\\text{–}10,\\ 8\\text{–}11,\\ 9\\text{–}10,\\ 9\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}12,\\ 10\\text{–}13,\\ 12\\text{–}13,\n\\end{aligned}$$\nand take $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 6$, $q = 0$. Then:\n\n- $S$ is a state, $O(S) = N[2] = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $b(S) = 3 \\le 6$;\n- $o(0, S) = \\{0, 7, 11\\}$, so $\\mathrm{open}(0, S) = 3$, and\n  $\\mathrm{cost}(S, 0) = |\\{0, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11\\}| = 6$, so $0$ is playable;\n- the customers $d \\notin S$ with $o(d, S) \\subseteq \\{0, 7, 11\\}$ are $0$,\n  $3$ and $4$ (both of the latter have $o = \\{0\\}$), so\n  $\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge \\mathrm{open}(0, S)$;\n- $0$ is the first customer in index order, so the code keeps $\\{0\\}$ alone;\n- $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(S)$ holds: the order $1, 3, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$\n  costs at most 6;\n- the child is $X = \\{0, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(X)$ fails: only seven\n  sets are reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none of them is\n  $C$.\n\n![The counterexample to Theorem 1](figures/definite_counterexample.png)\n\n*Figure 4.1. The graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the state where the definite\nmove fails. Customer 2 is closed, and 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens the\nnew stacks 0, 7 and 11, and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the rule\nkeeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them: closing\nthem first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution starting\nwith 0 cannot match. Regenerate with `python -m paper2.counterexample_figure`,\nwhich recomputes every labelled fact from the instance (PDF beside it).*\n\nSo Theorem 1 is false, both as published and as coded. (Lean:\n`definiteMove_counterexample`, `cexGraph`, `cex_solvable`, and\n`cexFamily_closed` for the seven sets, checked by `decide +kernel`.) The published implication itself is refuted as a universal statement, with\nthe bridge from the free-closed child to $S \\cup \\{q\\}$ inside the proof,\nunder the code's reading of $\\mathrm{close}$ and under the literal one, which\nalso counts closed customers and so is a weaker premise\n(`chuStuckey_theorem1_false`, `chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal`,\n`Search/PublishedTheorems.lean`). The same two theorems refute the thesis's\nTheorem 6.3.6, restated under its number as `chuThesis_theorem636_false` and\n`chuThesis_theorem636_false_literal` (`Search/ChuThesis.lean`). Fink (2012,\npp. 27–28) restates the rule with a stronger premise: it counts only dominated\ncustomers of smaller index than $q$, so not $q$ itself, and needs the child to\nhave strictly fewer open stacks than the parent. The graph of Counterexample 4.5\ndoes not refute it under any labelling, but adding a third twin of $3$ and $4$\ngives a 15-customer graph that refutes it (Lean: `fink_theorem1_false`;\n`python3 -m paper2.fink_check`).\n\n**Why the published proof fails.** The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\nsolution $U' = S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [c_1, \\dots, c_m, q, \\dots]$ and claims that\nafter each prefix \"we have at most open(q, S) extra stacks open, but at least\nclose(q, S) extra stacks closed\" (PDF p. 6). Compare the two runs at a prefix\n$T \\supseteq S$ of $U'$ that does not yet contain $q$. Moving $q$ forward\nchanges the open stacks from $b(T)$ to $b(T \\cup X)$, and\n$$b(T \\cup X) - b(T) = |N[q] \\setminus O(T)| - |X \\setminus T|.$$\nAt $T = S$ this is $\\mathrm{open} - \\mathrm{close} \\le 0$. But the customers of\n$X$ that $U'$ has *already* closed are in $T$, so they are no longer extra\nstacks closed, while the stacks $q$ opens may still be new. In the example,\n$U'$ may begin with $3$, after which $4$ is free and $T = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$. Then\n$N[0] \\setminus O(T) = \\{7, 11\\}$ and $X \\setminus T = \\{0\\}$, so moving $0$\nforward costs one more stack: $b(T) = 2$ and $b(T \\cup X) = 3$. Customers $3$\nand $4$ each counted once in $\\mathrm{close}(0, S)$, but they share the single\nnew stack $0$, and a solution that closes them first gains two closed stacks\nfor one opened.\n\nThere is a second, independent slip. Even where the conclusion holds, the\nliteral sequence $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q, c_1, \\dots]$ need not be a solution,\nbecause a customer that $q$ makes free stays open until its turn comes. With\nedges $0\\text{–}3$ and $1\\text{–}2$, $S = \\emptyset$ and $q = 0$\n($\\mathrm{open} = \\mathrm{close} = 2$), the order $1, 2, 0, 3$ costs 2 but\n$0, 1, 2, 3$ costs 3. Closing $3$ right after $0$ fixes it. The argument needs\nthe free moves.\n\n**Repair.** Call $q \\notin S$ *hereditarily definite* at $S$ when\n$$b(X) \\le b(B) \\quad \\text{for every } B \\text{ with } S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X \\text{ and } q \\notin B.$$\nBy Lemma 4.4, the code's premise is the case $B = S$. The repair asks that no\nintermediate set avoiding $q$ is a better place to stand than $X$ (Lean:\n`IsHereditarilyDefinite`; `IsHereditarilyDefinite.isDefinite`). In the\ncounterexample it fails at $B = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$, where $b(B) = 2 < 3 = b(X)$\n(Lean: `not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex`).\n\n**Lemma 4.6 (open stacks are submodular).** For all $A, B \\subseteq C$,\n$$b(A \\cup B) + b(A \\cap B) \\le b(A) + b(B).$$\n\n*Proof.* Since $T \\subseteq O(T)$, $b(T) = |O(T)| - |T|$. Now\n$O(A \\cup B) = O(A) \\cup O(B)$ and $O(A \\cap B) \\subseteq O(A) \\cap O(B)$, so\n$|O(A \\cup B)| + |O(A \\cap B)| \\le |O(A)| + |O(B)|$. Subtract\n$|A \\cup B| + |A \\cap B| = |A| + |B|$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n`openStacks_submodular`, from `openStacks_add_card`.)\n\n*In plain English.* Count the stacks opened by two groups of closed customers.\nThe union of the groups opens exactly what either opens, and their overlap\nopens at most what both open. So the two counts on the left can only fall\nshort of the two on the right, and subtracting the customers themselves, who\nbalance exactly, keeps that.\n\n**Theorem 4.7 (the repaired definite move is sound).** If $q \\notin S$ is\nhereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(X)$, with no other\nhypothesis. At a state with the invariant,\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, so keeping only a\nplayable hereditarily definite $q$ is sound.\n\n*Proof.* Take a closing order $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ from $S$ of cost at most $k$,\nwith prefixes $T_i = S \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$, and let $c_j = q$. Build an\norder from $X$ by following $c_1, \\dots, c_{j-1}$ and skipping any customer\nalready in $X$. For $i < j$ with $c_i \\notin X$, the step closes $c_i$ at\n$T_{i-1} \\cup X$ and costs $b(T_i \\cup X) + 1$. Put $Z = T_i$. Then\n$S \\subseteq Z \\cap X \\subseteq X$ and $q \\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n$b(X) \\le b(Z \\cap X)$, and Lemma 4.6 gives\n$$b(Z \\cup X) \\le b(Z) + b(X) - b(Z \\cap X) \\le b(Z).$$\nSo the step costs at most $b(T_i) + 1 = \\mathrm{cost}(T_{i-1}, c_i) \\le k$.\nAfter these steps the closed set is $T_{j-1} \\cup X$. It contains $T_j$, and\nsince $S \\subseteq T_{j-1}$ its opened set is $O(T_{j-1}) \\cup N[q] = O(T_j)$.\nThe rest of the original order gives $P_k(T_j)$, so Lemma 4.1 gives\n$P_k(T_{j-1} \\cup X)$, and together $P_k(X)$.\n\nFor the search's form, the invariant and Lemma 4.2 give $P_k(S)$, hence\n$P_k(X)$, hence $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(X)) = \\mathrm{Sol}_k(X)$ by Lemma 4.2\nagain. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`, by\n`solvable_union_cl_of_hereditarilyDefinite` and `stepCost_union_cl_le`;\n`searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`.)\n\n*In plain English.* Take any good closing order and imagine closing $q$, with\neverything it makes free, before anything else. At each moment before the\norder reaches $q$, compare the two situations. The hereditary condition says\nthat standing on the \"$q$ and its free customers\" side never leaves more stacks\nopen than standing at any point between where you started and there, provided\n$q$ itself has not closed. Submodularity turns that into a step-by-step\nguarantee: the order with $q$ first never has more stacks open than the\noriginal. Once the original order closes $q$, the two have the same stacks\nopen and the shifted one has closed at least as much.\n\n**Corollary.** Every $q$ with $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le 1$ is hereditarily\ndefinite, so the code's rule is right in those cases. For\n$S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X$ with $q \\notin B$, the stacks in $O(X)$ but not in\n$O(B)$ lie in $N[q] \\setminus O(S) = o(q, S)$, so there is at most one of\nthem. Since $O(B) \\subseteq O(X)$, $b(X) - b(B) = |O(X) \\setminus O(B)| -\n|X \\setminus B| \\le 1 - 1 = 0$, because $q \\in X \\setminus B$. (Lean: `isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one`.)\n\n**The matching form.** The repair quantifies over all sets $B$. An equivalent\nform is cheap. Let $Y = X \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$, the customers the child\ncloses besides $q$, and for $D \\subseteq Y$ let\n$o(D) = \\bigcup_{d \\in D} o(d, S)$. Every $d \\in Y$ has\n$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$, so consider the bipartite graph joining each\n$d \\in Y$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n\n**Theorem 4.8 (the repair is a matching condition).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n$q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff some set $M \\subseteq Y$ with\n$|M| \\ge \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ can be matched injectively to stacks\n$f(d) \\in o(d, S)$. Equivalently, the largest matching of the bipartite graph\nhas at least $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ edges.\n\n*Proof.* Let $q \\notin S$; if $q \\in S$ then $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) = 0$ and both\nsides hold trivially. The sets $B$ of the repair are exactly $B = S \\cup D$\nwith $D \\subseteq Y$. Since $|O(S \\cup D)| = |O(S)| + |o(D)|$ and\n$|X| = |S| + 1 + |Y|$,\n$$b(X) \\le b(S \\cup D) \\iff \\mathrm{open}(q, S) + |D| \\le |o(D)| + |Y| + 1 \\iff |D| - |o(D)| \\le \\delta,$$\nwith $\\delta = |Y| + 1 - \\mathrm{open}(q, S)$. (At $D = \\emptyset$ this gives\n$\\delta \\ge 0$.) By the deficiency form of Hall's theorem (Ore 1955), the\nlargest matching of $Y$ into the stacks has size\n$|Y| - \\max_{D \\subseteq Y} (|D| - |o(D)|)$. So the repair holds iff that\nmaximum is at most $\\delta$, iff the largest matching has size at least\n$|Y| - \\delta = \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$. $\\square$\n\nThe Lean proof uses the textbook reduction of the deficiency form to Hall's\ntheorem: give every $d \\in Y$ the same $\\delta$ dummy stacks besides $o(d, S)$,\ncheck Hall's condition from the deficiency inequality, apply Mathlib's\n`Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_existsInjective'`, and keep the customers\nmatched to real stacks, of which at most $\\delta$ are lost. (Lean:\n`HasDefiniteMatching`, `card_opened_union_eq`, `card_cl_insert`,\n`hall_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`, `exists_matching_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`,\n`isHereditarilyDefinite_of_matching`, and\n`isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`.)\n\n*In plain English.* Closing $q$ opens some new stacks, and it lets some other\ncustomers close for free, each of whom needed one or more of those new stacks.\nThe rule is safe exactly when you can pair all but one of the new stacks with\na different free customer who needed it. In the counterexample, closing $0$\nopens three new stacks, $0$, $7$ and $11$, and frees customers $3$ and $4$.\nBoth of them needed only stack $0$, so only one pairing is possible where two\nare required.\n\n### 4.3.3 The subset rule\n\n**Published form.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can\nalways play i before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will\nclose i in any case. Hence move j can be removed from the candidates R\nconsidered for the next customer to close\" (§2, PDF p. 5).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** Say $d$ *dominates* $r$ at $S$ when $d \\ne r$,\n$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, and either the inclusion is strict or $d < r$.\nThe rule drops every $r \\in P$ dominated by some $d \\in C \\setminus S$, where\n$d$ ranges over all remaining customers, including those in $Q$. If every\ncandidate would be dropped, it keeps $P$ whole (the *fallback*). It runs only\nwhen the definite move did not fire (Lean: `Dominates`, `IsSubsetDominated`,\n`subsetFilter`).\n\n**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$\nfor the dominator $d$. **Sound as coded.**\n\n**Lemma 4.9 (the covering).** If $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$ and\n$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, then\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$, for every set\n$S$, with no invariant and no tie-break. Moreover $d$ is playable.\n\n*Proof.* $N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup o(d, S) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, so\n$d \\in S \\cdot r$, and $O(S \\cup \\{d\\}) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ gives\n$S \\cdot d \\subseteq S \\cdot r$ and $\\mathrm{cost}(S, d) \\le \\mathrm{cost}(S, r)$.\nIf $r \\in S \\cdot d$, the two opened sets are equal and $S \\cdot d = S \\cdot r$.\nOtherwise close $r$ at $S \\cdot d$. It costs\n$|O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot d| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S| =\n\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, and it lands on\n$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S \\cup \\{r\\})) = S \\cdot r$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n`searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`, with\n`mem_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n`cl_insert_subset_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n`cl_insert_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n`stepCost_le_of_newlyOpened_subset`.)\n\n**Proposition 4.10 (node soundness).** At any state $S \\ne C$, if every\n$q \\in Q$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, the subset filter is node-sound.\nWhen a solution exists, the fallback never fires.\n\n*Proof.* Domination is irreflexive and transitive, so it is a strict order.\nGiven $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, some playable $r$ has $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r)$,\nand $r \\notin Q$. Among the customers $m \\notin S$ with\n$o(m, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, take the least by $(|o(m, S)|, m)$. Any dominator\nof $m$ would come earlier in that order, so $m$ is undominated. By Lemma 4.9,\n$m$ is playable and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot m)$ holds, so $m \\notin Q$. So $m$\nis a candidate that survives. $\\square$ (Lean: `not_dominates_self`,\n`Dominates.trans`, `exists_undominated`, `subsetKept_nonempty`,\n`subsetFilter_sound`.)\n\n*In plain English.* If closing $d$ opens only stacks that closing $r$ would\nopen anyway, then closing $r$ makes $d$ free. So closing $d$ first, then $r$,\nreaches the same place as closing $r$, never at a higher cost, and the search\nmay as well try $d$. Follow such domination downwards and you reach a customer\nnobody dominates, which the rule keeps.\n\n**The tie-break is necessary (Counterexample 4.11).** Without it, two\ncustomers with equal new stacks dominate each other and both go. Take the\n7-customer graph with edges $0\\text{–}2$, $0\\text{–}3$, $0\\text{–}6$,\n$1\\text{–}4$, $1\\text{–}6$, $3\\text{–}6$, $4\\text{–}5$, $4\\text{–}6$, the\nstate $S = \\{2\\}$ and $k = 3$. The playable candidates are $\\{0, 3, 5\\}$, with\n$o(0, S) = o(3, S) = \\{3, 6\\}$ and $o(5, S) = \\{4, 5\\}$. Without the tie-break\n$0$ and $3$ both go, $5$ survives, so the fallback does not fire, and the\nfilter keeps $\\{5\\}$. A solution exists from $S$ ($0, 3, 1, 4, 5, 6$), but none\nfrom $S \\cdot 5 = \\{2, 5\\}$, where no move costs at most 3. With the tie-break,\nonly $3$ goes and the filter keeps $\\{0, 5\\}$. (Lean:\n`noTieBreak_counterexample`, `tieGraph`, `WeakDominates`, `weakSubsetFilter`.)\nThe paper's own form, which requires $d < r$ even for a strict inclusion, is a\nsubrelation of the code's, uses the same covering and is acyclic by index. It\nis checked, not stated in Lean.\n\n### 4.3.4 The better move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 2.** Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are\nplayable and close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is\na solution there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.2, PDF p. 6). The\nproof begins: \"The conditions imply that if r is played now, q becomes a\ndefinite move.\"\n\n**Precise form, as coded** (the published premises; in the C since 2026-09-26, in\nthe Python since 2026-10-01, and under `repaired_rules=False` since then). The input is the\nlist $W$ of subset survivors, in index order. Let $X_r = O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. A\nmember $r$ is dropped if some earlier $q \\in W$, among the first $L$ of $W$ if\n$L > 0$, satisfies\n\n- *(premise 3)* $|(X_r \\cup N[q]) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k$, and\n- *(premise 4)* $\\mathrm{open}' \\le \\mathrm{close}'$, where\n  $\\mathrm{open}' = |N[q] \\setminus X_r|$ and $\\mathrm{close}'$ counts the\n  $d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus X_r \\subseteq N[q] \\setminus X_r$.\n\nPremise 2 is $q \\in W \\subseteq P$. Whether $q$ itself survives is not\nconsulted. (Lean: `IsBetter`, `betterCite`, `withinLimit`, `betterFilterBy`.)\n\n**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n\n**Lemma 4.12 (the premise is Theorem 1 at the child).** Premises 3 and 4 hold\niff $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q) \\le k$ and the code's definite premise\nholds for $q$ at the child $T = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$.\n\n*Proof.* Premise 3 is $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q)$ by definition. Since\n$O(T) = X_r$, $\\mathrm{open}' = \\mathrm{open}(q, T)$. A customer\n$d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ has $N[d] \\setminus X_r \\ne \\emptyset$ exactly when\n$d \\notin T$, and then $N[d] \\setminus X_r = o(d, T)$. So\n$\\mathrm{close}' = \\mathrm{close}(q, T)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `isBetter_iff`,\n`betterOpen_eq`, `betterClose_eq`.)\n\nSo the better move is Theorem 1 at the child, followed by a swap of $q$ and\n$r$. The swap is sound. The appeal to Theorem 1 is not.\n\n**Counterexample 4.13.** Take the graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the root,\n$S = \\emptyset$, with $k = 6$ and $Q = \\emptyset$. No playable customer meets\nthe definite premise, so the definite move does not fire. Customers $0$ and $2$\nboth survive the subset rule, $0 < 2$, and premises 3 and 4 hold for $r = 2$,\n$q = 0$, so the code drops $2$ citing $0$. But $S \\cdot 2 = \\{2\\}$ has a\nsolution (Counterexample 4.5), and $S \\cdot 0 = \\{0\\}$ has none. The child\n$\\{2\\}$ is exactly the state at which Theorem 1 wrongly moves $0$ forward.\n(Lean: `betterMove_counterexample`, by an invariant family of 18 sets,\n`bmFamily_closed`.) Theorem 2's published implication is refuted the same way, under both\nreadings of $\\mathrm{close}$ (`chuStuckey_theorem2_false`,\n`chuStuckey_theorem2_false_literal`). At this node the filter still keeps customer $1$, which has\na solution, so this is a *false link*, not a lost node (Lean:\n`betterMove_counterexample_node`). No node where the better move loses the last\nsolution has been found (section 4.5).\n\n**The thesis's form.** Chu (2011) states the better move as \"**Theorem 6.3.8.**\nSuppose S is some sequence, and q, r ∉ S are customers such that: S ++ [q] and\nS ++ [r, q] are both k-playable, and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}). If\nS ++ [r] has an extension that uses ≤ k stacks, then S ++ [q] also has an\nextension that uses ≤ k stacks\" (p. 143). The premise takes close at $S$, not at\n$S \\cup \\{r\\}$. The proof derives the CP premise from it\n(\"close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}), so after r is played, q\nbecomes a definite move\") and then appeals to Theorem 6.3.6, so it fails at the\nsame step. Counterexample 4.13 does not cover it, but the graph of\nCounterexample 4.5 does: take $S = \\{2\\}$, $r = 3$, $q = 0$, $k = 6$. Then\n$\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge 2 = \\mathrm{open}(0, S \\cup \\{3\\})$, both\n$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3, 0]$ are playable (costs 6\nand 5), $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3]$ extends by\n$1, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$, and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ has no\nextension, which is Counterexample 4.5's child. Under the literal reading of\nclose the premise is weaker and the same witness serves; a second witness,\n$S = \\{1\\}$, $r = 6$, $q = 12$, holds under the literal reading only.\n(Lean: `chuThesis_theorem638_false`, `chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal`,\n`chuThesis_theorem638_literal_witness`, `Search/ChuThesis.lean`; the brute\nforce over all $2^{14}$ sets that found the witnesses is\n`python3 paper2/thesis_check.py`: 13 failing (state, $r$, $q$) triples under\nthe code's reading, 22 under the literal one.)\n\n**Repair.** Cite $q$ for $r$ only if premise 3 holds and $q$ is hereditarily\ndefinite at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ (Lean: `IsRepairedBetter`;\n`IsRepairedBetter.isBetter` shows it implies the code's premise). By Theorem\n4.8 this is one matching at the child.\n\n**Theorem 4.14 (the repaired better move is sound).** If $r, q \\notin S$,\n$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$ and the repaired premise holds, then\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n\n*Proof.* Let $T = S \\cdot r$, which has the invariant because $r$ is playable.\nIf $q \\in T$, put $T' = T$. Otherwise Theorem 4.7 at $T$ gives\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(T')$ for $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T \\cup \\{q\\})$. Now let\n$Z = S \\cdot q$. Both $\\mathrm{cl}(Z \\cup \\{r\\})$ and $T'$ equal\n$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[r])$. If $r \\in Z$ then $Z = T'$. Otherwise\nclosing $r$ at $Z$ costs\n$$|O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus Z| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| = |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k,$$\nwhere the middle equality exchanges $q$ and $r$, both of which are opened and\nneither of which is in $S$, and the last step is premise 3. So $r$ is a\nplayable move from $Z$ to $T'$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(Z)$ follows. $\\square$\n(Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`,\n`searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`, with `card_sdiff_insert_eq`.) The\nimplication needs $r$ playable, not $q$. Playability of $q$ is what makes $q$ a\nchild of the search.\n\n*In plain English.* The better move says: \"if closing $r$ now would make $q$ a\nsafe next move, then closing $q$ first and $r$ second is at least as good as\nclosing $r$ first.\" The swap at the end is fine, because both orders end in the\nsame place and neither step overflows. What fails is \"would make $q$ a safe\nnext move\", which was Theorem 1. With the repaired test there, the argument\ngoes through unchanged.\n\n**Two earlier faults, now proved.** The 2026-09-26 fix corrected two bugs in\nthis rule, both caught by testing (`../reports/better_move_bug.md` §7). Lean\nnow proves a counterexample to each.\n\n- *Bug A, the close count.* Counting also the customers that $r$ finishes on\n  its own makes the rule's own conclusion false, on a 12-customer graph at\n  $k = 4$ (Lean: `IsBetterOld`, `bugA_counterexample`, `bugAGraph`).\n- *Bug B, the order of the rules.* Running the better move before the subset\n  rule lets the subset rule cite a customer the better move has already\n  dropped. On an 8-customer graph at $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 4$, the better move drops\n  $3$ citing $0$ and the subset rule drops $0$ citing $3$. Every premise is the\n  corrected one and true, and the node is lost through the cycle (Lean:\n  `oldOrderFilter`, `bugB_counterexample`, `bugBGraph`).\n\n### 4.3.5 The old move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 3.** Let S = [s₁, s₂, …, sₙ]. Suppose that\nS′ = [s₁, s₂, .., sₘ, q, sₘ₊₁, …, sₙ] is playable, then if U′ = S ++ [q] ++ R is\na solution then U = S′ ++ R is a solution\" (§3.3, PDF p. 7), with the rule\n\"if it is found that at some ancestor node, the q branch has been searched and\nU is playable, then q can immediately be pruned\".\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** A node carries a set $Q$ of old moves, empty at the\nroot. When a candidate's subtree returns *false* without aborting, the\ncandidate joins the node's list `seen`. A later sibling $c'$ passes to its child\nthose $q \\in$ `seen` with\n$$\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c') = |(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c']) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k,$$\nwhich is the test that the step $c'$ stays playable with $q$ reinserted before\nit. Old moves are removed from the candidates (Lean: `Inherits`, `pathEnd`).\n\n**Claimed soundness.** Every $q \\in Q$ at a node has\n$\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. **Sound as coded.**\n\n**Lemma 4.15 (reinsertion).** If $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c) \\le k$, then\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\nfor every set $S$, with no invariant. Iterated along a path whose every move\npasses the test: if the $q$ branch at an ancestor has no solution, neither has\n$q$ at the end of the path.\n\n*Proof.* Both $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)$ and\n$\\mathrm{cl}(\\{c\\} \\cup S \\cdot q)$ equal $\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c])$.\nIf $c \\in S \\cdot q$, then the second is $S \\cdot q$ itself. Otherwise $c$ is a\nmove at $S \\cdot q$ of cost\n$|O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot q| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k$,\nbecause $S \\cup \\{q\\} \\subseteq S \\cdot q$. Induction on the path gives the\nsecond statement. $\\square$ (Lean: `searchSol_reinsert`,\n`cl_insert_cl_insert_comm`, `stepCost_cl_le`, `searchSol_reinsert_path`,\n`not_searchSol_of_oldMove`.)\n\n**The test is necessary.** On the path $4\\text{–}0\\text{–}2\\text{–}1\\text{–}3$\nwith $S = \\emptyset$, $q = 2$, $c = 3$ and $k = 2$, the move $c$ is playable but\n$\\mathrm{cost}(\\{2\\}, 3) = 3$. Here\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{2\\} \\cup \\mathrm{cl}\\{3\\})) = \\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{1, 2, 3\\})$\nholds and $\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{2\\})$ does not. This is the smallest such graph,\nby exhaustion to 5 vertices (Lean: `reinsert_needs_test`). Chu & Stuckey make\nthe same point: the condition \"is in fact crucial\" (PDF p. 7).\n\n*In plain English.* Suppose that at some earlier node the search tried closing\n$q$ and found no way to finish. Since then it has closed some other customers.\nIf, at every one of those steps, closing $q$ first would still have kept the\nstep within budget, then \"close $q$ now\" leads to a place the search could also\nhave reached from the earlier attempt. That attempt failed, so this one fails\ntoo, and $q$ can be skipped.\n\nThe paper's \"synergy\" (adding $r$ to $Q$ when an old move is better than $r$)\nis implemented in neither the C nor the Python and is not covered.\n\n### 4.3.6 The memo\n\n**Published form.** Nogood recording: \"if it failed, we record the nogood and\nreturn false when we revisit it\" (PDF p. 4).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** When a node's loop finishes with no child answering\n*true* and without an abort, its closed set $S$ is recorded. At any later node\nwith the same closed set the search answers *false* at once. The memo may\nstore fewer entries (a limit, a load factor), which only stores less.\n\n**Claimed soundness.** A recorded $S$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n\n**Lemma 4.16 (the key).** If $b(T) \\le k$, $b(T') \\le k$ and\n$\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{cl}(T')$, then $P_k(T) \\iff P_k(T')$. So a state\nrefuted once is refuted whatever path reached it.\n\n*Proof.* Lemma 4.2 at both sides. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_of_cl_eq`.)\n\nThe memo is sound once every recorded entry is a genuine refutation. With old\nmove on, that is not a property of the node alone, and it is proved for the\nwhole run in section 4.4.2.\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 The composition and the theorem\n\n### 4.4.1 The filter\n\nThe filter runs in the order\n\n$$\\text{definite move} \\;\\to\\; \\text{subset rule} \\;\\to\\; \\text{better move},$$\n\neach once, and each cites only customers still standing or in $Q$. If the\ndefinite move fires, $L = [q]$. Otherwise the subset rule runs over $P$ with\ndominators from all of $C \\setminus S$, while nothing has yet been discarded.\nThe better move then runs over the subset survivors and cites only an earlier\nsurvivor (Lean: `fullFilter`; `codeFullFilter` with the code's premises,\n`repairedFullFilter` with both repairs).\n\n**Proposition 4.17 (the repaired filter is node-sound).** At every state with\nthe invariant, for every limit $L$ and every set $Q$ of refuted old moves,\n$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$ with both repairs is\nnode-sound.\n\n*Proof.* If the definite move fires on a playable hereditarily definite $q$,\nTheorem 4.7 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Otherwise Proposition 4.10 gives\na subset survivor with a solution. Let $w$ be the earliest such survivor in\nindex order. If the better move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor $q$\nunder the repaired premise, and Theorem 4.14 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\nwhich contradicts the choice of $w$. So $w \\in L$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n`betterFilterBy_sound`, `fullFilter_sound`, `repairedFilter_sound`,\n`repairedFullFilter_sound`.)\n\nThe argument needs no chain of citations and does not use $L$. Bug B shows\nthat the order matters. With the better move first, a subset link can point\nbackwards at a customer already discarded, and every premise can be true while\nthe chain cycles.\n\n*In plain English.* If the safe-move test fires, the one move it keeps is\nproved safe. If it does not, the subset rule always keeps some move that still\nleads to a solution. Among the moves that do, look at the first one. The better\nmove can only throw it out by pointing at an even earlier move that it proves\nat least as good, and there is none. So something that still works always\nsurvives.\n\n### 4.4.2 Runs, the memo and old move together\n\nA run of the search is described by a big-step semantics of its *false*\nanswers (Lean: `Exec`). A node intersects $Q$ with its remaining customers,\nenumerates the filter's output in any order, runs each child with any subset of\nthe old moves that pass the reinsertion test, adds each refuted child to\n`seen`, and records its state in the memo or not. A memo hit answers *false*.\nAn aborted run produces no `Exec` at all, which matches the code: an abort\nanswers *unknown* and records nothing. So the child order, the memo policy and\nold move on or off are all covered.\n\n**Theorem 4.18 (runs are sound).** Let the filter be node-sound and return only\nplayable customers. Suppose that at the start of a run the invariant holds at\n$S$, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and every $q \\in Q \\setminus S$\nhas $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Then the run leaves only genuine memo\nentries, and if it answers *false* then $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n\n*Proof.* Induct on the run, which follows the order in which subtrees complete.\nEach entry of `seen` at a node is either a sibling subtree that has already\ncompleted with *false*, refuted by the induction hypothesis, or one of the\nnode's own old moves, refuted by hypothesis. Each old move a child inherits\npassed the test, so Lemma 4.15 makes it genuinely refuted at the child. When a\nnode's loop has refuted every member of the filter's output, node soundness","numLines":813,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1141,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}}}
