{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0196kwGL9LynnbJkYQGpuCYr","type":"tool_result","content":"813\tnode's loop has refuted every member of the filter's output, node soundness\n814\tgives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, so recording $S$ is correct. A memo hit is\n815\tcorrect by the hypothesis on the memo. $\\square$ (Lean: `Exec.sound`,\n816\t`exec_root_sound`; `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` with\n817\t`repairedFullFilter_filterSound` and `repairedFullFilter_playable`.)\n818\t\n819\t**Why one implementation refused the combination.** The Python reference drops\n820\tthe memo under old move, on the ground that a failure reached with old-move\n821\tpruning depends on the branches an ancestor searched. What is true in this is\n822\tlocal: a node's run *taken on its own*, with an old move that is not refuted,\n823\tcan refute a solvable state and record it. One isolated customer with $k = 1$\n824\tand the old move $Q = \\{0\\}$ is enough (Lean: `exec_fake_oldMove`). But the\n825\thypothesis of Theorem 4.18 holds in every real run, because every old move is a\n826\trefutation completed earlier in the same run. So the combination is sound,\n827\twhich is what the C has always run and what every production refutation used.\n828\tWhat is lost is that a memo entry cannot be checked without re-deriving the\n829\tcited subtree's $Q$, which is why the certificate checker declines that\n830\tconfiguration.\n831\t\n832\t### 4.4.3 The theorem\n833\t\n834\t**Theorem 4.19 (the revised search is sound).** Run the customer search with\n835\tfree moves, the memo, old move and the filter\n836\t$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$, with the definite move\n837\tfiring only on a hereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only\n838\tunder the repaired premise, with any limit $L$, any child order and any memo\n839\tpolicy. If the run refutes $k$ from the root, then:\n840\t\n841\t| conclusion | Lean |\n842\t|---|---|\n843\t| no closing order costs at most $k$, $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` |\n844\t| $k < \\nu(G)$, the narrowness | `exec_repairedFullFilter_narrowness` |\n845\t| $\\mathrm{pw}(G) > k - 1$, for nonempty $C$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth` |\n846\t| on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a requirement, $Z(M) > k$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue` |\n847\t| the same in pathwidth form, $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospGraph_pathwidth` |\n848\t\n849\t*Proof.* Proposition 4.17 makes the filter node-sound, and it returns only\n850\tplayable customers. At the root the invariant holds, the memo is empty and\n851\t$Q = \\emptyset$, so Theorem 4.18 gives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$. Lemma 4.2\n852\tat the root turns this into $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$, and Theorem 4.3 turns it into\n853\t$\\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 > k$ and, on $G_M$, $Z(M) > k$. $\\square$\n854\t\n855\t*In plain English.* Each rule throws away only moves that some kept move is\n856\tproved to be at least as good as. The memo and the old-move list only ever\n857\tremember failures that really happened earlier in the same search. So when the\n858\tsearch says \"no\", every way of finishing was either tried or provably no better\n859\tthan one that was tried, and no closing order fits in $k$ stacks. By the\n860\tearlier sections, that means the instance needs more than $k$ stacks and its\n861\tgraph has pathwidth at least $k$.\n862\t\n863\t### 4.4.4 What holds for the code\n864\t\n865\t**The code runs the revised search.** Since 2026-10-01 (Ralph loop0007, items\n866\t01 to 03) the repaired rules are the default in both solvers: in\n867\t`satisfiability.customer_search.decide` and `satisfiability.native.decide_native`\n868\tfor MOSP, and in `pathwidth.search.decide` and `pathwidth.native.decide_native`\n869\tfor graphs, each with the flag `repaired_rules=True`. Every caller that does not\n870\tname the flag runs them, including `solve_mosp_exact`, `benchmarks.csearch`,\n871\t`benchmarks.recertify` and `pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`. The definite move\n872\tfires on the first playable $q$ in index order that passes the published test\n873\t$\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ and then the matching test of Theorem 4.8. A\n874\t$q$ that fails the matching is passed over, and a later candidate may fire, so\n875\tthe pick is `repairedFullFilter`'s. The better move cites $q$ for $r$ only under\n876\tpremises 3 and 4 and the same matching at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, which is\n877\t`IsRepairedBetter`. The free moves, the subset rule with its index tie-break,\n878\tthe order $\text{definite} \to \text{subset} \to \text{better}$, the old move\n879\twith its reinsertion test and the memo are unchanged, and they are the ones\n880\tproved sound as coded. So a refutation by the code is a refutation by the\n881\tsearch of Theorem 4.19, and gives $Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$,\n882\tas far as the code is that search.\n883\t\n884\t**That the code is that search is checked, not proved.** Lean models the\n885\trules; the C and the Python are read against the model and checked against it\n886\tby brute force (`solver_fix.md`, items 01 to 05):\n887\t\n888\t| check | scope | result |\n889\t|---|---|---|\n890\t| production filter against a transcription of the Lean `repairedFullFilter` | 16,244,090 node checks, graphs on 1–17 vertices, exhaustive to 6 | equal everywhere |\n891\t| the matching against the hereditary premise | 36,939,226 candidates | equal everywhere |\n892\t| C against Python, MOSP, both settings | 1,557,980 calls, 1,529 instances at up to 125 customers | equal in answer, nodes and witness |\n893\t| eight implementations of the graph solver (Python, the multiword C at 2–16 words, the single-word C, MOSP's Python and C) | 12,582,320 calls, 1,319 graphs at 4–992 vertices | equal |\n894\t| the whole revised search written from the theorems with the oracle's predicates, against the C, the Python and the oracle | 17,431,232 runs, 52,592 graphs at 1–17 vertices, all 64 configurations | zero answer failures, zero node losses in 51,454,712 nodes, C and Python equal node for node |\n895\t| differential harness (relabellings, re-coverings, both configurations, $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$ and $\\mathrm{opt}$) | every certified instance at 9–75 customers, 1,786,824 calls | zero disagreements |\n896\t\n897\tThe matching is Kuhn's augmenting paths, over at most 128 stacks in the\n898\tsingle-word C and $64 \\cdot \text{WORDS}$ in the multiword C. Three things still\n899\trun the published rules: the flag `repaired_rules=False`, kept for comparison;\n900\tthe C entry points kept for old processes (`cs_decide_variant`, `cs_decide_fan`,\n901\t`cs_decide`); and the certificate emitter (`learning/search_certificate.py`),\n902\twhich does not yet model the repairs. What the published rules give is the rest\n903\tof this section.\n904\t\n905\t**The published rules, as the code ran them until 2026-10-01.** They use the\n906\tunrepaired premises: $\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ for the definite move,\n907\tand premises 3 and 4 for the better move. This filter is **not** node-sound. On\n908\tthe graph of Counterexample 4.5 at $k = 6$, for every $L$, it keeps only $0$ at\n909\tthe state $\\{2\\}$, which has a solution, while $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{0, 2\\})$ has none\n910\t(Lean: `codeFullFilter_cex`). Against the named property `CodeFilterSound` (the\n911\tcode's filter is node-sound at every state of one instance at one $k$), this is\n912\t`not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph`.\n913\t\n914\tWhat holds for them is conditional, and the condition is named rather than\n915\tassumed.\n916\t\n917\t- **The gap, named.** `CodeRunSound` says that the code's run expands only\n918\t  nodes at which its filter keeps a solution whenever one exists. It is a\n919\t  hypothesis, not a theorem. Under it, a code refutation is genuine and gives\n920\t  $Z(M) > k$ (Lean: `ExecOn`, `ExecOn.sound`, `codeExec_sound_of_runSound`,\n921\t  `codeExec_pathwidth_of_runSound`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_runSound`).\n922\t- **A checkable condition at one node.** `CodeNodeRepaired` holds at a node\n923\t  when, if the definite move fires, its pick is hereditarily definite, and, if\n924\t  it does not fire, every customer the better move drops has *some* earlier\n925\t  cited survivor meeting the repaired premise. It implies node soundness\n926\t  (Lean: `nodeSoundAt_codeFullFilter_of_repaired`). A run of the code whose\n927\t  every expanded node passes it is sound and gives $Z(M) > k$ (Lean:\n928\t  `codeExec_sound_of_repaired`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`). This is what\n929\t  a certificate would have to carry to certify a refutation by the unrepaired\n930\t  code.\n931\t\n932\t**Why a false refutation is hard to produce.** Every node the search expands is\n933\treached from the root by playable moves. If the root has no solution, no\n934\texpanded node has one, and every expanded node is node-sound for free. So a\n935\tfalse refutation needs a $k$ at which a solution exists, together with a lost\n936\tnode on every branch that leads to a solution.\n937\t\n938\t**Lost nodes do occur in real runs.** In the check of section 4.5, a run under\n939\tthe published rules reaches a node where its filter loses the last solution in 58 of\n940\t570,206 runs. All 58 are on graphs built around the definite-move\n941\tcounterexample (29 graphs, each at two values of $L$), all at\n942\t$k = \\mathrm{opt}$ and never at $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$, as the previous paragraph\n943\trequires. The smallest has 14 customers. Every one of the 58 runs found a\n944\tsolution through another branch and answered correctly. In 26 of them the lost\n945\tsubtree joined `seen` and passed a non-refuted old move to its siblings, at 83\n946\tnodes in all, which is the cascade Theorem 4.18's hypothesis excludes. It did\n947\tnot change an answer. Over the same runs, `CodeNodeRepaired` held at\n948\t1,781,000 of 1,782,100 expanded nodes and failed at 1,100, most of which still\n949\tkept a solution.\n950\t\n951\t**The refutations made under the published rules, audited** (`solver_fix.md`,\n952\titems 06 and 07). Of the 6,374 certified values in the corpus, 115 (113\n953\tdistinct graphs, 40–125 customers, all Chu & Stuckey `Random` instances plus SP3\n954\tand SP4) had no evidence for the refutation of $\\text{value} - 1$ outside the\n955\tcustomer search under the published rules. A C audit mode re-runs those\n956\tsearches as they were run and evaluates `CodeNodeRepaired` at every expanded\n957\tnode, without changing the search. Of the 103 that finish in 1,200 s, **33\n958\tpass at every node**, so they are sound as run, by\n959\t`codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`, independently of the repaired code. **70 do\n960\tnot**: they fail at 0.11% of their nodes, mostly at the definite move and, in\n961\tall 8 runs with Theorem 2, at the better move too. A failing node is one where\n962\tthe repaired premise does not hold, not one where a solution was lost. All 70\n963\tare re-refuted by the repaired solver. So for most of these values the\n964\tevidence is the repaired refutation, not the old one.\n965\t\n966\t**The certificate checker.** The search certificate\n967\t(`learning/search_certificate.py`) checks each pruning step against a premise.\n968\tFor the free moves, the memo, the subset rule, the covering condition and the\n969\told moves, the premise it checks is the one the Lean lemma needs. For the\n970\tdefinite and better steps it checks the published premises, which\n971\tCounterexamples 4.5 and 4.13 show can certify a false link, and its emitter\n972\tmodels only the published rules. A certificate whose definite and better steps\n973\tmeet the repaired premises proves $Z(M) > k$ with no trust in the search, by\n974\t`nodeSoundAt_of_covering` at each node, then `ExecOn.sound`, then Theorem 4.19's\n975\tchain. Item 07 of `solver_fix.md` does this check outside the checker: it walks\n976\t58 emitted certificates of old refutations with its own matching and finds 32\n977\tthat meet the repaired premises at every node. That the checker's Python\n978\tcomputes the quantities named is a reading of the code, backed by tests, and not\n979\tproved.\n980\t\n981\t**Open.** Whether the published rules ever answer *unsat* wrongly on a whole\n982\tinstance. None is known. None of the 115 values above changed when re-refuted.\n983\tThe only open question about the solvers as they now run is the reading that\n984\tthe code is the search of Theorem 4.19, which the checks above support and do\n985\tnot prove.\n986\t\n987\t---\n988\t\n989\t## 4.5 The evidence\n990\t\n991\tEvery statement above was checked by brute force before it was proved.\n992\t`paper2/search_check.py` implements the model and the rules from the written\n993\tstatements, sharing no code with the solver, and compares them with an exact\n994\toracle for $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$ computed over all states. The first check (item 06)\n995\tcovered every labelled graph on 1 to 6 vertices (33,867 graphs), every graph on\n996\t7 vertices under the identity and 24 random labellings (26,100), 6,000 random\n997\tgraphs on 8 to 16 vertices, and three pinned instances, at every $k$. For the\n998\trules as coded it found zero failures of node soundness, of cited links, of the\n999\tcovering condition and of whole-search answers, over 111,288,536, 90,915,044\n1000\tand 355,181,036 node checks in the three families. It also confirmed that both\n1001\tpre-fix forms of the better move fail: Bug A's conclusion first fails at 12\n1002\tvertices, and Bug B first loses a node at 8. The same runs went through the C\n1003\tsolver, and **the port matched the C in answer and node count on all 4,418,084\n1004\truns**, including the faulty variants. That is what shows the rules of section\n1005\t4.3 are the rules the C runs.\n1006\t\n1007\tThat check did not see Theorem 1's fault. The smallest graph on which the\n1008\tdefinite move loses the last solution has 14 customers. There is none on any\n1009\tgraph to 9 vertices (exhaustive) and none on the 88,802 connected graphs on 10\n1010\tvertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was found by a hunt in a\n1011\t\"gadget\" family built from the failed proof, and minimised by deletion to 14\n1012\tcustomers and 26 edges, never fewer. Under the repair and its matching form the\n1013\thunt found zero losses in 2.05 G premise checks. A port of the search with the\n1014\tcode's definite move gave no false refutation at the optimum on 600,000 random\n1015\tgraphs at 10 to 18 vertices and 90,000 gadget graphs at 12 to 17. The final\n1016\tcheck (item 12) ran 570,206 searches with memo and old move: the repaired\n1017\tsearch agreed with the oracle on every one, and the code gave zero wrong\n1018\tanswers. Each later item has its own check table in `search_soundness.md`\n1019\t§4.1.4 to §5.4, all with zero failures for the proved statements.\n1020\t\n1021\t---\n1022\t\n1023\t## 4.6 What changes in practice\n1024\t\n1025\t### 4.6.1 The revised algorithm\n1026\t\n1027\tThe search is unchanged except for two tests. Below, $\\mathrm{MaxMatch}(S, q)$\n1028\tis the size of a largest matching between the customers\n1029\t$Y = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$ and the stacks\n1030\t$o(q, S)$, joining $d$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n1031\t\n1032\t```\n1033\tfilter(S, Q):                                   # S a state, Q the old moves\n1034\t  P := [c not in S ∪ Q : cost(S, c) ≤ k], in index order\n1035\t  for q in P:                                   # definite move\n1036\t    if open(q, S) ≤ close(q, S)                 #   the published test\n1037\t       and MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1:     #   the repair (Theorem 4.8)\n1038\t      return [q]\n1039\t  W := [r in P : no d in C∖S dominates r]       # subset rule, index tie-break\n1040\t  if W is empty: W := P                         #   fallback\n1041\t  kept := []\n1042\t  for r in W, in index order:                   # better move\n1043\t    T := cl(S ∪ {r})\n1044\t    drop := exists an earlier q in W (among the first L if L > 0) with\n1045\t              |(O(T) ∪ N[q]) ∖ (S ∪ {r})| ≤ k   #   premise 3\n1046\t              and open(q, T) ≤ close(q, T)      #   premise 4, Theorem 2's test\n1047\t              and MaxMatch(T, q) ≥ open(q, T) − 1   # the repair at the child\n1048\t    if not drop: append r to kept\n1049\t  return kept\n1050\t```\n1051\t\n1052\tThe free moves, the memo, the old moves with their reinsertion test, the cost\n1053\tcut and the child order are as before. Keeping the published test in front of\n1054\tthe matching is only a shortcut, since the repair implies it.\n1055\t\n1056\tThis is what both solvers run by default since 2026-10-01 (section 4.4.4). In\n1057\tthe code, `MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1` is `_has_definite_matching` in\n1058\t`satisfiability/customer_search.py` and `has_definite_matching` in\n1059\t`customer_search.c` and its two counterparts in `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/`\n1060\t(`closing_search.c`, a byte copy, and the multiword `closing_search_w.c`). All\n1061\tstop at `open − 1` edges and return at once when `open ≤ 1`. In the code the better\n1062\tmove's freed set at the child is the customers $d \\ne q$ outside\n1063\t$S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\subseteq\n1064\tN[q] \\setminus O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. `repaired_rules=False` gives the published\n1065\tfilter.\n1066\t\n1067\t### 4.6.2 Its cost\n1068\t\n1069\tThe repair adds one bipartite matching per candidate that passes the published\n1070\ttest, and nothing for the others. The matching is small: it joins at most\n1071\t$\\mathrm{close} - 1$ customers to $\\mathrm{open}$ stacks, and it can stop as\n1072\tsoon as it has $\\mathrm{open} - 1$ edges. Every candidate with\n1073\t$\\mathrm{open} \\le 1$ passes at once (Corollary to Theorem 4.7). In the better\n1074\tmove the same matching is computed at the child $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$,\n1075\tonce per pair $(r, q)$ that passes premises 3 and 4. By Theorem 4.8 the test is\n1076\texact: where it fails, the repaired premise fails, and a solution may avoid\n1077\t$q$. So no sound pruning that the repaired theorems allow is given up.\n1078\t\n1079\tMeasured in paired runs, the same decision under both rule sets in one worker\n1080\t(`solver_fix.md`, items 04 and 08). Nodes are totals over the pairs that\n1081\tfinished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost.\n1082\t\n1083\t| where | size | pairs finished | nodes, repaired / published |\n1084\t|---|---|---|---|\n1085\t| MOSP corpus, refuting $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$ | 9–40 customers | 6,135 of 6,135 (two configurations) | 1.00003, 1.00004 |\n1086\t| Chu & Stuckey classes | 50–100 customers | 118 of 125 | 1.0030 (worst class `100-50-4`, 1.010; worst pair 1.013) |\n1087\t| Chu & Stuckey classes, to $2 \\times 10^8$ nodes | 125 × 125 | 11 of 23 | 0.9999 |\n1088\t| graph pathwidth, whole descents | 22 to over 1,000 vertices | 731 of 880 graphs | 1.020 |\n1089\t| graph benchmarks rerun at the first sweep's caps, graphs refuted by both runs | up to 957 vertices | 8,456 | 1.003–1.017 per set; median pair 1.000, worst 1.64 |\n1090\t\n1091\tPer node, measured quietly on six hard instances at 75–125 customers, the\n1092\trepaired rules cost about 2% in the MOSP C (from −0.1% to +5.8%). In the graph\n1093\tsolver's descents, measured under load, they cost about 6%. The published test\n1094\tpasses and the matching then fails on 0.07–0.27% of definite-move candidates\n1095\t(the rate rises with size: 0.07–0.11% at 9–40 customers, 0.13% at 50–100,\n1096\t0.27% at 125 × 125 to the node cap) and on 0.02–0.06% of better-move pairs. The definite move\n1097\tstops firing at a node on 0.007–0.08% of filter calls; elsewhere a later\n1098\tcandidate fires instead. No answer differed between the two settings in any of\n1099\tthese runs, and every width the graph benchmarks had proved was proved again.\n1100\t\n1101\tNot measured: the tree size of the longest refutations (`Random-100-100-2` and\n1102\tthe 125 × 125 instances at densities 2 and 4) under the published rules, which\n1103\twere censored on both sides. There the per-node cost and the counter rates are\n1104\tthe evidence.\n1105\t\n1106\t### 4.6.3 What was redone, and what is left\n1107\t\n1108\t- **The solvers: done** (2026-10-01). The definite move and the better move\n1109\t  are repaired in the C customer search, in its Python reference (which also\n1110\t  gained the better move, until then C-only) and in the graph pathwidth solver\n1111\t  (`../pathwidth_solver/`), as the owner decided. The repaired rules are the\n1112\t  default in both. Section 4.4.4 lists the checks.\n1113\t- **Re-certification: 108 of 115 done, 7 left.** Values certified by the\n1114\t  subset-lattice oracle, by a checked DRAT refutation through the SAT path, by\n1115\t  the SAT binary search before the customer search existed, or by a lower bound\n1116\t  equal to the value, do not depend on the search and stand as they are (6,259\n1117\t  of the 6,374 certified values). The other 115 rested on the customer search\n1118\t  under the published rules. The repaired solver re-refutes $\\text{value} - 1$\n1119\t  on 108 of them (106 of 113 distinct graphs; every one at 40–100 customers and\n1120\t  16 of 23 at 125 × 125), in $9.73 \\times 10^{10}$ nodes. **No value changed.**\n1121\t  The 7 left are 125 × 125 instances at densities 2 and 4\n1122\t  (`Random-125-125-2-{1,4,5}_0`, `Random-125-125-4-{1,2,4,5}_0`), censored\n1123\t  after 10.1 hours each at $5.0$–$6.9 \\times 10^{10}$ nodes and priced at 13 to\n1124\t  153 core-hours each, which is probably low. They keep their values as\n1125\t  verified upper bounds. For them the refutation of $\\text{value} - 1$ under\n1126\t  the repaired rules is still to be made, and none of the old runs passes the\n1127\t  audit of section 4.4.4. A resumable runner continues them\n1128\t  (`solver_fix.md`, item 07, \"Needs a long run\").\n1129\t- **The certificate route: left.** The certificate checker's definite and\n1130\t  better steps must check the repaired premises, and the emitter must model the\n1131\t  repaired rules: one matching per step. With that change, an accepted\n1132\t  certificate proves $Z(M) > k$ by Theorem 4.19's chain without trusting the\n1133\t  search, which is the route this paper's dataset relies on for values the SAT\n1134\t  path cannot reach. Item 07's walk is a first form of the check, run outside\n1135\t  the checker.\n1136\t- **A faster route for the 7, proposed and not built.** The repaired filter is\n1137\t  node-sound at the root, and by Theorem 4.18 each root child can be refuted in\n1138\t  its own process from its own state with no old moves and an empty memo. That\n1139\t  spreads one refutation over many cores. It needs a C entry point that starts\n1140\t  from a given state, and a short Lean statement of the composition.\n1141\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3e233eac-acc6-45da-bdf4-c3a7e4de0805","uuid":"0f03e913-34ec-4c40-bbf8-bb0bca7cb1f6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T01:12:51.590Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/revised_algorithm.md","content":"node's loop has refuted every member of the filter's output, node soundness\ngives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, so recording $S$ is correct. A memo hit is\ncorrect by the hypothesis on the memo. $\\square$ (Lean: `Exec.sound`,\n`exec_root_sound`; `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` with\n`repairedFullFilter_filterSound` and `repairedFullFilter_playable`.)\n\n**Why one implementation refused the combination.** The Python reference drops\nthe memo under old move, on the ground that a failure reached with old-move\npruning depends on the branches an ancestor searched. What is true in this is\nlocal: a node's run *taken on its own*, with an old move that is not refuted,\ncan refute a solvable state and record it. One isolated customer with $k = 1$\nand the old move $Q = \\{0\\}$ is enough (Lean: `exec_fake_oldMove`). But the\nhypothesis of Theorem 4.18 holds in every real run, because every old move is a\nrefutation completed earlier in the same run. So the combination is sound,\nwhich is what the C has always run and what every production refutation used.\nWhat is lost is that a memo entry cannot be checked without re-deriving the\ncited subtree's $Q$, which is why the certificate checker declines that\nconfiguration.\n\n### 4.4.3 The theorem\n\n**Theorem 4.19 (the revised search is sound).** Run the customer search with\nfree moves, the memo, old move and the filter\n$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$, with the definite move\nfiring only on a hereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only\nunder the repaired premise, with any limit $L$, any child order and any memo\npolicy. If the run refutes $k$ from the root, then:\n\n| conclusion | Lean |\n|---|---|\n| no closing order costs at most $k$, $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` |\n| $k < \\nu(G)$, the narrowness | `exec_repairedFullFilter_narrowness` |\n| $\\mathrm{pw}(G) > k - 1$, for nonempty $C$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth` |\n| on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a requirement, $Z(M) > k$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue` |\n| the same in pathwidth form, $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospGraph_pathwidth` |\n\n*Proof.* Proposition 4.17 makes the filter node-sound, and it returns only\nplayable customers. At the root the invariant holds, the memo is empty and\n$Q = \\emptyset$, so Theorem 4.18 gives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$. Lemma 4.2\nat the root turns this into $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$, and Theorem 4.3 turns it into\n$\\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 > k$ and, on $G_M$, $Z(M) > k$. $\\square$\n\n*In plain English.* Each rule throws away only moves that some kept move is\nproved to be at least as good as. The memo and the old-move list only ever\nremember failures that really happened earlier in the same search. So when the\nsearch says \"no\", every way of finishing was either tried or provably no better\nthan one that was tried, and no closing order fits in $k$ stacks. By the\nearlier sections, that means the instance needs more than $k$ stacks and its\ngraph has pathwidth at least $k$.\n\n### 4.4.4 What holds for the code\n\n**The code runs the revised search.** Since 2026-10-01 (Ralph loop0007, items\n01 to 03) the repaired rules are the default in both solvers: in\n`satisfiability.customer_search.decide` and `satisfiability.native.decide_native`\nfor MOSP, and in `pathwidth.search.decide` and `pathwidth.native.decide_native`\nfor graphs, each with the flag `repaired_rules=True`. Every caller that does not\nname the flag runs them, including `solve_mosp_exact`, `benchmarks.csearch`,\n`benchmarks.recertify` and `pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`. The definite move\nfires on the first playable $q$ in index order that passes the published test\n$\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ and then the matching test of Theorem 4.8. A\n$q$ that fails the matching is passed over, and a later candidate may fire, so\nthe pick is `repairedFullFilter`'s. The better move cites $q$ for $r$ only under\npremises 3 and 4 and the same matching at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, which is\n`IsRepairedBetter`. The free moves, the subset rule with its index tie-break,\nthe order $\text{definite} \to \text{subset} \to \text{better}$, the old move\nwith its reinsertion test and the memo are unchanged, and they are the ones\nproved sound as coded. So a refutation by the code is a refutation by the\nsearch of Theorem 4.19, and gives $Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$,\nas far as the code is that search.\n\n**That the code is that search is checked, not proved.** Lean models the\nrules; the C and the Python are read against the model and checked against it\nby brute force (`solver_fix.md`, items 01 to 05):\n\n| check | scope | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| production filter against a transcription of the Lean `repairedFullFilter` | 16,244,090 node checks, graphs on 1–17 vertices, exhaustive to 6 | equal everywhere |\n| the matching against the hereditary premise | 36,939,226 candidates | equal everywhere |\n| C against Python, MOSP, both settings | 1,557,980 calls, 1,529 instances at up to 125 customers | equal in answer, nodes and witness |\n| eight implementations of the graph solver (Python, the multiword C at 2–16 words, the single-word C, MOSP's Python and C) | 12,582,320 calls, 1,319 graphs at 4–992 vertices | equal |\n| the whole revised search written from the theorems with the oracle's predicates, against the C, the Python and the oracle | 17,431,232 runs, 52,592 graphs at 1–17 vertices, all 64 configurations | zero answer failures, zero node losses in 51,454,712 nodes, C and Python equal node for node |\n| differential harness (relabellings, re-coverings, both configurations, $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$ and $\\mathrm{opt}$) | every certified instance at 9–75 customers, 1,786,824 calls | zero disagreements |\n\nThe matching is Kuhn's augmenting paths, over at most 128 stacks in the\nsingle-word C and $64 \\cdot \text{WORDS}$ in the multiword C. Three things still\nrun the published rules: the flag `repaired_rules=False`, kept for comparison;\nthe C entry points kept for old processes (`cs_decide_variant`, `cs_decide_fan`,\n`cs_decide`); and the certificate emitter (`learning/search_certificate.py`),\nwhich does not yet model the repairs. What the published rules give is the rest\nof this section.\n\n**The published rules, as the code ran them until 2026-10-01.** They use the\nunrepaired premises: $\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ for the definite move,\nand premises 3 and 4 for the better move. This filter is **not** node-sound. On\nthe graph of Counterexample 4.5 at $k = 6$, for every $L$, it keeps only $0$ at\nthe state $\\{2\\}$, which has a solution, while $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{0, 2\\})$ has none\n(Lean: `codeFullFilter_cex`). Against the named property `CodeFilterSound` (the\ncode's filter is node-sound at every state of one instance at one $k$), this is\n`not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph`.\n\nWhat holds for them is conditional, and the condition is named rather than\nassumed.\n\n- **The gap, named.** `CodeRunSound` says that the code's run expands only\n  nodes at which its filter keeps a solution whenever one exists. It is a\n  hypothesis, not a theorem. Under it, a code refutation is genuine and gives\n  $Z(M) > k$ (Lean: `ExecOn`, `ExecOn.sound`, `codeExec_sound_of_runSound`,\n  `codeExec_pathwidth_of_runSound`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_runSound`).\n- **A checkable condition at one node.** `CodeNodeRepaired` holds at a node\n  when, if the definite move fires, its pick is hereditarily definite, and, if\n  it does not fire, every customer the better move drops has *some* earlier\n  cited survivor meeting the repaired premise. It implies node soundness\n  (Lean: `nodeSoundAt_codeFullFilter_of_repaired`). A run of the code whose\n  every expanded node passes it is sound and gives $Z(M) > k$ (Lean:\n  `codeExec_sound_of_repaired`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`). This is what\n  a certificate would have to carry to certify a refutation by the unrepaired\n  code.\n\n**Why a false refutation is hard to produce.** Every node the search expands is\nreached from the root by playable moves. If the root has no solution, no\nexpanded node has one, and every expanded node is node-sound for free. So a\nfalse refutation needs a $k$ at which a solution exists, together with a lost\nnode on every branch that leads to a solution.\n\n**Lost nodes do occur in real runs.** In the check of section 4.5, a run under\nthe published rules reaches a node where its filter loses the last solution in 58 of\n570,206 runs. All 58 are on graphs built around the definite-move\ncounterexample (29 graphs, each at two values of $L$), all at\n$k = \\mathrm{opt}$ and never at $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$, as the previous paragraph\nrequires. The smallest has 14 customers. Every one of the 58 runs found a\nsolution through another branch and answered correctly. In 26 of them the lost\nsubtree joined `seen` and passed a non-refuted old move to its siblings, at 83\nnodes in all, which is the cascade Theorem 4.18's hypothesis excludes. It did\nnot change an answer. Over the same runs, `CodeNodeRepaired` held at\n1,781,000 of 1,782,100 expanded nodes and failed at 1,100, most of which still\nkept a solution.\n\n**The refutations made under the published rules, audited** (`solver_fix.md`,\nitems 06 and 07). Of the 6,374 certified values in the corpus, 115 (113\ndistinct graphs, 40–125 customers, all Chu & Stuckey `Random` instances plus SP3\nand SP4) had no evidence for the refutation of $\\text{value} - 1$ outside the\ncustomer search under the published rules. A C audit mode re-runs those\nsearches as they were run and evaluates `CodeNodeRepaired` at every expanded\nnode, without changing the search. Of the 103 that finish in 1,200 s, **33\npass at every node**, so they are sound as run, by\n`codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`, independently of the repaired code. **70 do\nnot**: they fail at 0.11% of their nodes, mostly at the definite move and, in\nall 8 runs with Theorem 2, at the better move too. A failing node is one where\nthe repaired premise does not hold, not one where a solution was lost. All 70\nare re-refuted by the repaired solver. So for most of these values the\nevidence is the repaired refutation, not the old one.\n\n**The certificate checker.** The search certificate\n(`learning/search_certificate.py`) checks each pruning step against a premise.\nFor the free moves, the memo, the subset rule, the covering condition and the\nold moves, the premise it checks is the one the Lean lemma needs. For the\ndefinite and better steps it checks the published premises, which\nCounterexamples 4.5 and 4.13 show can certify a false link, and its emitter\nmodels only the published rules. A certificate whose definite and better steps\nmeet the repaired premises proves $Z(M) > k$ with no trust in the search, by\n`nodeSoundAt_of_covering` at each node, then `ExecOn.sound`, then Theorem 4.19's\nchain. Item 07 of `solver_fix.md` does this check outside the checker: it walks\n58 emitted certificates of old refutations with its own matching and finds 32\nthat meet the repaired premises at every node. That the checker's Python\ncomputes the quantities named is a reading of the code, backed by tests, and not\nproved.\n\n**Open.** Whether the published rules ever answer *unsat* wrongly on a whole\ninstance. None is known. None of the 115 values above changed when re-refuted.\nThe only open question about the solvers as they now run is the reading that\nthe code is the search of Theorem 4.19, which the checks above support and do\nnot prove.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 The evidence\n\nEvery statement above was checked by brute force before it was proved.\n`paper2/search_check.py` implements the model and the rules from the written\nstatements, sharing no code with the solver, and compares them with an exact\noracle for $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$ computed over all states. The first check (item 06)\ncovered every labelled graph on 1 to 6 vertices (33,867 graphs), every graph on\n7 vertices under the identity and 24 random labellings (26,100), 6,000 random\ngraphs on 8 to 16 vertices, and three pinned instances, at every $k$. For the\nrules as coded it found zero failures of node soundness, of cited links, of the\ncovering condition and of whole-search answers, over 111,288,536, 90,915,044\nand 355,181,036 node checks in the three families. It also confirmed that both\npre-fix forms of the better move fail: Bug A's conclusion first fails at 12\nvertices, and Bug B first loses a node at 8. The same runs went through the C\nsolver, and **the port matched the C in answer and node count on all 4,418,084\nruns**, including the faulty variants. That is what shows the rules of section\n4.3 are the rules the C runs.\n\nThat check did not see Theorem 1's fault. The smallest graph on which the\ndefinite move loses the last solution has 14 customers. There is none on any\ngraph to 9 vertices (exhaustive) and none on the 88,802 connected graphs on 10\nvertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was found by a hunt in a\n\"gadget\" family built from the failed proof, and minimised by deletion to 14\ncustomers and 26 edges, never fewer. Under the repair and its matching form the\nhunt found zero losses in 2.05 G premise checks. A port of the search with the\ncode's definite move gave no false refutation at the optimum on 600,000 random\ngraphs at 10 to 18 vertices and 90,000 gadget graphs at 12 to 17. The final\ncheck (item 12) ran 570,206 searches with memo and old move: the repaired\nsearch agreed with the oracle on every one, and the code gave zero wrong\nanswers. Each later item has its own check table in `search_soundness.md`\n§4.1.4 to §5.4, all with zero failures for the proved statements.\n\n---\n\n## 4.6 What changes in practice\n\n### 4.6.1 The revised algorithm\n\nThe search is unchanged except for two tests. Below, $\\mathrm{MaxMatch}(S, q)$\nis the size of a largest matching between the customers\n$Y = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$ and the stacks\n$o(q, S)$, joining $d$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n\n```\nfilter(S, Q):                                   # S a state, Q the old moves\n  P := [c not in S ∪ Q : cost(S, c) ≤ k], in index order\n  for q in P:                                   # definite move\n    if open(q, S) ≤ close(q, S)                 #   the published test\n       and MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1:     #   the repair (Theorem 4.8)\n      return [q]\n  W := [r in P : no d in C∖S dominates r]       # subset rule, index tie-break\n  if W is empty: W := P                         #   fallback\n  kept := []\n  for r in W, in index order:                   # better move\n    T := cl(S ∪ {r})\n    drop := exists an earlier q in W (among the first L if L > 0) with\n              |(O(T) ∪ N[q]) ∖ (S ∪ {r})| ≤ k   #   premise 3\n              and open(q, T) ≤ close(q, T)      #   premise 4, Theorem 2's test\n              and MaxMatch(T, q) ≥ open(q, T) − 1   # the repair at the child\n    if not drop: append r to kept\n  return kept\n```\n\nThe free moves, the memo, the old moves with their reinsertion test, the cost\ncut and the child order are as before. Keeping the published test in front of\nthe matching is only a shortcut, since the repair implies it.\n\nThis is what both solvers run by default since 2026-10-01 (section 4.4.4). In\nthe code, `MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1` is `_has_definite_matching` in\n`satisfiability/customer_search.py` and `has_definite_matching` in\n`customer_search.c` and its two counterparts in `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/`\n(`closing_search.c`, a byte copy, and the multiword `closing_search_w.c`). All\nstop at `open − 1` edges and return at once when `open ≤ 1`. In the code the better\nmove's freed set at the child is the customers $d \\ne q$ outside\n$S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\subseteq\nN[q] \\setminus O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. `repaired_rules=False` gives the published\nfilter.\n\n### 4.6.2 Its cost\n\nThe repair adds one bipartite matching per candidate that passes the published\ntest, and nothing for the others. The matching is small: it joins at most\n$\\mathrm{close} - 1$ customers to $\\mathrm{open}$ stacks, and it can stop as\nsoon as it has $\\mathrm{open} - 1$ edges. Every candidate with\n$\\mathrm{open} \\le 1$ passes at once (Corollary to Theorem 4.7). In the better\nmove the same matching is computed at the child $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$,\nonce per pair $(r, q)$ that passes premises 3 and 4. By Theorem 4.8 the test is\nexact: where it fails, the repaired premise fails, and a solution may avoid\n$q$. So no sound pruning that the repaired theorems allow is given up.\n\nMeasured in paired runs, the same decision under both rule sets in one worker\n(`solver_fix.md`, items 04 and 08). Nodes are totals over the pairs that\nfinished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost.\n\n| where | size | pairs finished | nodes, repaired / published |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MOSP corpus, refuting $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$ | 9–40 customers | 6,135 of 6,135 (two configurations) | 1.00003, 1.00004 |\n| Chu & Stuckey classes | 50–100 customers | 118 of 125 | 1.0030 (worst class `100-50-4`, 1.010; worst pair 1.013) |\n| Chu & Stuckey classes, to $2 \\times 10^8$ nodes | 125 × 125 | 11 of 23 | 0.9999 |\n| graph pathwidth, whole descents | 22 to over 1,000 vertices | 731 of 880 graphs | 1.020 |\n| graph benchmarks rerun at the first sweep's caps, graphs refuted by both runs | up to 957 vertices | 8,456 | 1.003–1.017 per set; median pair 1.000, worst 1.64 |\n\nPer node, measured quietly on six hard instances at 75–125 customers, the\nrepaired rules cost about 2% in the MOSP C (from −0.1% to +5.8%). In the graph\nsolver's descents, measured under load, they cost about 6%. The published test\npasses and the matching then fails on 0.07–0.27% of definite-move candidates\n(the rate rises with size: 0.07–0.11% at 9–40 customers, 0.13% at 50–100,\n0.27% at 125 × 125 to the node cap) and on 0.02–0.06% of better-move pairs. The definite move\nstops firing at a node on 0.007–0.08% of filter calls; elsewhere a later\ncandidate fires instead. No answer differed between the two settings in any of\nthese runs, and every width the graph benchmarks had proved was proved again.\n\nNot measured: the tree size of the longest refutations (`Random-100-100-2` and\nthe 125 × 125 instances at densities 2 and 4) under the published rules, which\nwere censored on both sides. There the per-node cost and the counter rates are\nthe evidence.\n\n### 4.6.3 What was redone, and what is left\n\n- **The solvers: done** (2026-10-01). The definite move and the better move\n  are repaired in the C customer search, in its Python reference (which also\n  gained the better move, until then C-only) and in the graph pathwidth solver\n  (`../pathwidth_solver/`), as the owner decided. The repaired rules are the\n  default in both. Section 4.4.4 lists the checks.\n- **Re-certification: 108 of 115 done, 7 left.** Values certified by the\n  subset-lattice oracle, by a checked DRAT refutation through the SAT path, by\n  the SAT binary search before the customer search existed, or by a lower bound\n  equal to the value, do not depend on the search and stand as they are (6,259\n  of the 6,374 certified values). The other 115 rested on the customer search\n  under the published rules. The repaired solver re-refutes $\\text{value} - 1$\n  on 108 of them (106 of 113 distinct graphs; every one at 40–100 customers and\n  16 of 23 at 125 × 125), in $9.73 \\times 10^{10}$ nodes. **No value changed.**\n  The 7 left are 125 × 125 instances at densities 2 and 4\n  (`Random-125-125-2-{1,4,5}_0`, `Random-125-125-4-{1,2,4,5}_0`), censored\n  after 10.1 hours each at $5.0$–$6.9 \\times 10^{10}$ nodes and priced at 13 to\n  153 core-hours each, which is probably low. They keep their values as\n  verified upper bounds. For them the refutation of $\\text{value} - 1$ under\n  the repaired rules is still to be made, and none of the old runs passes the\n  audit of section 4.4.4. A resumable runner continues them\n  (`solver_fix.md`, item 07, \"Needs a long run\").\n- **The certificate route: left.** The certificate checker's definite and\n  better steps must check the repaired premises, and the emitter must model the\n  repaired rules: one matching per step. With that change, an accepted\n  certificate proves $Z(M) > k$ by Theorem 4.19's chain without trusting the\n  search, which is the route this paper's dataset relies on for values the SAT\n  path cannot reach. Item 07's walk is a first form of the check, run outside\n  the checker.\n- **A faster route for the 7, proposed and not built.** The repaired filter is\n  node-sound at the root, and by Theorem 4.18 each root child can be refuted in\n  its own process from its own state with no old moves and an empty memo. That\n  spreads one refutation over many cores. It needs a C entry point that starts\n  from a given state, and a short Lean statement of the composition.\n","numLines":329,"startLine":813,"totalLines":1141}}}
