{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019WMyjJwnq6ogukCH9nfzyc","type":"tool_result","content":"                                                                          \u0002\nwe denote by N G− (U ) the set of in-neighbors of U , i.e., N G− (U ) = v∈U N G− (v)\\U .\nWe denote in-degrees |N G− (v)| and |N G− (U )| by dG− (v) and dG− (U ) respectively. When\nG is clear from the context, we drop G from this notation. We also use the notation\nŨ = V (G)\\(U ∪ N − (U )) where G is implicit.\n   The following fact is well-known and is straightforward to prove.\nProposition 1 The integer valued function d − on 2V (G) is submodular. That is, for\nevery pair X, Y ⊆ V (G), we have d − (X ) + d − (Y ) ≥ d − (X ∪ Y ) + d − (X ∩ Y ).\n   When we consider a sequence σ of vertices of G in this paper, σ will always be such\nthat each vertex of the graph occurs at most once in σ . For each sequence σ of vertices\nof G, we denote by V (σ ) the set of vertices constituting σ and by |σ | = |V (σ )| the\nlength of σ . A sequence σ of vertices of G is a permutation if V (σ ) = V (G). For\nbrevity, we often write d − (σ ) and N − (σ ) for d − (V (σ )) and N − (V (σ )), respectively.\n   For each pair of sequences σ and τ of vertices of G such that V (σ ) ∩ V (τ ) = ∅,\nwe denote by σ τ the sequence that is σ followed by τ . If σ = σ τ for some τ , then\nwe say that σ is a prefix of σ and that σ is an extension of (or extends) σ ; we say\nthat σ is a proper prefix of σ and that σ is a proper extension of σ if τ is nonempty.\nWe call σ a subsequence of τ , if V (σ ) ⊆ V (τ ) and moreover the order in which the\nvertices of σ appear in τ is the same as in σ .\n   Let G be a digraph and k a positive integer. We say a sequence σ of vertices of G\nis k-feasible for G if d − (σ ) ≤ k for every prefix σ of σ . We say that σ is strongly\nk-feasible for G if σ is a prefix of a k-feasible permutation. Clearly, if σ is strongly\nk-feasible then it is k-feasible. We may drop the reference to G and say σ is k-feasible\n(or strongly k-feasible) when G is clear from the context. These feasibility notions\n\n                                                                                  123\n\f142                                                              Algorithmica (2016) 75:138–157\n\n\nare defined for vertex sets as well. For each U ⊆ V (G), we say that U is k-feasible\n(strongly k-feasible) if there is a k-feasible (strongly k-feasible) sequence σ with\nV (σ ) = U .\n   The directed vertex separation number of digraph G, denoted by dvsn(G), is the\nminimum integer k such that V (G) is k-feasible.\n   It is known that the directed pathwidth of G equals dvsn(G) for every digraph G\n[26] (see also [15] for the undirected case). Based on this fact, we work on the directed\nvertex separation number in the remaining of this paper.\n   Let H (x) = −x log x − (1 − x) log(1 − x), 0 < x < 1, denote the binary entropy\nfunction. We freely use the following well-known bound on the number of subsets of\nbounded cardinality.\n\nProposition 2 (see [9], for example) Let S be a set of n elements and let 0 < α ≤ 21 .\nThen the number of subsets of S with cardinality at most αn is at most 2 H (α)n .\n\n\n3 Commitments and Related Lemmas\n\nWe use several techniques to reduce the search space for a k-feasible permutation. They\ncan be treated in a unified manner based on a general principle called commitments in\n[25].\n   Let σ be a k-feasible sequence and let τ be a k-feasible proper extension of σ . We\ncall τ a k-committable extension of σ if, for every X with V (σ ) ⊆ X ⊆ V (τ ), we have\nd − (X ) ≥ d − (τ ). We extend this notion to vertex sets: W ⊆ V (G) is a k-committable\nextension of U ⊆ V (G) if there are sequences σ and τ with U = V (σ ) and W = V (τ )\nsuch that τ is a k-committable extension of σ .\n   The following lemma states that, if a k-feasible sequence σ has a k-committable\nextension τ , then, in search for a k-feasible permutation that extends σ , the only\nextensions of σ to be considered are the extensions of τ . In other words, we may let\nthe search node σ commit to τ .\n\nLemma 1 (Commitment Lemma [25]) Let G be a digraph and k a positive integer.\nLet σ be a k-feasible sequence and let τ be a k-committable extension of σ . If σ is\nstrongly k-feasible then so is τ .\n\nProof Let σ and τ be as above and suppose σ is strongly k-feasible. Let η be a k-\nfeasible permutation that extends σ . Let α be the subsequence of η such that V (α) =\nV (G)\\V (τ ). Our goal is to show that the permutation τ α is k-feasible.\n   For 1 ≤ i ≤ |α|, let αi denote the prefix of α of length i and let ηi denote the\nmaximal prefix of η such that V (ηi ) ∩ V (α) = V (αi ). Fix i, 1 ≤ i ≤ |α|. By the\nsubmodularity of d − , we have\n\n           d − (τ ) + d − (ηi ) ≥ d − (V (τ ) ∩ V (ηi )) + d − (V (τ ) ∪ V (ηi )).\n\nSince V (σ ) is disjoint from V (α), ηi is an extension of σ and hence V (σ ) ⊆ V (τ ) ∩\nV (ηi ) ⊆ V (τ ). Therefore, by the definition of k-committable extensions, we have\nd − (V (τ ) ∩ V (ηi )) ≥ d − (τ ) and hence\n\n123\n\fAlgorithmica (2016) 75:138–157                                                         143\n\n\n                                 d − (τ αi ) = d − (V (τ ) ∪ V (ηi ))\n                                           ≤ d − (ηi ).\n\nSince d − (ηi ) ≤ k as η is k-feasible, we have d − (τ αi ) ≤ k. Since this holds for every\ni, 1 ≤ i ≤ |α|, τ α is k-feasible.\n\nCorollary 1 Let G be a digraph and k a positive integer. Let U be a k-feasible subset\nof V (G) and let W be a k-committable extension of U . If U is strongly k-feasible then\nso is W .\n\n   The XP algorithm in Sect. 7 uses k-committable extensions in general form, while\nour main algorithms in Sect. 4 and Sect. 5 directly use a few special cases of k-\ncommittable extensions introduced below.\n   Following the terminology of [24] for the undirected case, we call U ⊆ V (G) a full\nset (with respect to G), if there is no v ∈ N − (U ) with N − (v) ⊆ U ∪ N − (U ). For each\nU , there is a unique superset of U that is a full set, which we denote by fullset(U ).\nIndeed, fullset(U ) is defined to be U ∪ Y where\n\n                     Y = {v ∈ N − (U ) | N − (v) ⊆ U ∪ N − (U )}.\n\nProposition 3 Let U ⊆ V (G) be k-feasible. Then, either fullset(U ) = U or\nfullset(U ) is a k-committable extension of U .\n\nProof Suppose fullset(U ) is a proper superset of U . Let σ be an arbitrary k-feasible\nsequence with V (σ ) = U and let τ be an arbitrary extension of σ with V (τ ) =\nfullset(U ). For each extension η of σ that is a prefix of τ , we have N − (η) ⊆ N − (U ).\nIt follows that τ is k-feasible. For each X such that U ⊆ X ⊆ fullset(U ), we\nhave N − (X ) = N − (U )\\X ⊇ N − (U ) \\ fullset(U ) = N − (fullset(U )) and there-\nfore d − (X ) ≥ d − (fullset(U )). This verifies that, by Lemma 1, τ is a k-committable\nextension of U .\n\n   Let U ⊆ V (G) and let H = G[V (G)\\N − (U )] be the subgraph of G induced by\nthe complement of N − (U ). Observe that, for each strongly connected component C\nof H , either C ⊆ U or C ⊆ Ũ , as H does not contain an edge from Ũ to U . Let C(H )\ndenote the set of all strongly connected components of H and consider the natural\npartial ordering ≺ on C(H ): C ≺ D if and only if C \u000f= D and H contains a directed\npath from a vertex in C to a vertex in D.\n\nLemma 2 Let U and H be as above and let C be a strongly connected component of\nH contained in Ũ minimal with respect to ≺, that is, C ∈ C(H ), C ⊆ Ũ , and there\nis no D ∈ C(H ) with D ⊆ Ũ and D ≺ C. Suppose furthermore that U is k-feasible\nand d − (U ) + |C| ≤ k + 1. Then, U ∪ C is a k-committable extension of U . Moreover,\ngiven a k-feasible sequence σ with V (σ ) = U , we can find a k-commitable extension\nτ of σ with V (τ ) = U ∪ C in polynomial time.\n\nProof Let σ be an arbitrary k-feasible sequence with V (σ ) = U and let τ be an\narbitrary extension of σ with V (τ ) = U ∪ C. Observe that the minimality condition\n\n                                                                                123\n\f144                                                         Algorithmica (2016) 75:138–157\n\n\nfor C implies that N − (U ∪ C) = N − (U ). For each proper extension η of σ that\nis a prefix of τ , we have N − (η) ⊆ N − (U ) ∪ (C\\V (η)) and therefore d − (η) ≤\nd − (U ) + |C| − 1 ≤ k. Therefore, τ is k-feasible. Moreover, for each Y ⊆ C, we\nhave d − (U ∪ Y ) ≥ d − (U ∪ C) since N − (U ∪ Y ) contains N − (U ) = N − (U ∪ C).\nTherefore, by Lemma 1, τ is a k-committable extension of σ .\n   This lemma together with the commitment lemma implies that, under the assump-\ntions of the lemma, U ∪ C is strongly k-feasible if U is. The undirected counterpart\nof this observation is called the component push rule in [24].\n   Based on the partial ordering ≺ on C(H ) defined above, we call a subset D of C(H )\ndownward closed if C ∈ D implies D ∈ D for every D ≺ C.\nProposition 4 Let U ⊆ V (G) and let H = G[V (G)\\N − (U )]. Then, the set of all\nstrongly connected components of H contained in U is a downward closed subset of\nC(H ).\n                                                                      \u0002\n   For each U ⊆ V (G) with d − (U ) ≤ k, we define pushk (U ) = C∈D C, where\nD is the maximal downward closed subset of C(H ) that avoids every C ∈ C(H )\nwith C ⊆ Ũ and d − (U ) + |C| > k + 1. Since the set of all strongly connected\ncomponents of H contained in U is downward closed by Proposition 4, we have\nU ⊆ pushk (U ). Algorithmically, pushk (U ) can be obtained as the value of W after\nthe following iteration: let W = U and, as long as the subset of C(H ) consisting of all\nthe components contained in W̃ has a minimal element C with d − (U ) + |C| ≤ k + 1,\nupdate W to be W ∪C. By a repeated application of Lemma 2, we obtain the following\nlemma.\nLemma 3 Let U ⊆ V (G). If U is k-feasible then so is pushk (U ). Moreover, if U is\nstrongly k-feasible then so is pushk (U ). Moreover, given a k-feasible sequence σ with\nV (σ ) = U , we can find a k-commitable extension τ of σ with V (τ ) = pushk (U ) in\npolynomial time when pushk (U ) is a proper superset of U .\n   Thus, our algorithm may focus only on those subsets U with pushk (U ) = U . The\nfollowing lemma is used to bound the number of such subsets.\nLemma 4 Let S ⊆ V (G) with |S| ≤ k. Then, the number of vertex sets U ⊆ V (G)\n                                                   n\nwith N − (U ) = S and pushk (U ) = U is at most 2 s+1 where s = k − |S| + 1.\nProof Fix S ⊆ V (G) with |S| ≤ k and let s = k − |S| + 1. Let H = G[V (G)\\S]\n=====\n                                      ≤n·               = O(n · 1.9633n ).\n                      i=1\n                              i                 0.404n\n\nFor type 2, the number is at most\n        0.192n\n         X \u0012       \u0013                  \u0012       \u0013\n                 n                        n\n                     · 2(n−i)/3 ≤ n ·           · 2(n−0.192n)/3 = O(n · 1.9657n ).\n         i=1\n                 i                     0.192n\n                                                                                     t\n                                                                                     u\n      Our goal now will be to compute the pathwidth of G in O∗ (|B|) time.\n\n4.2     Extending from the base sets\nThe set of pairs contained in B at this point will be referred to as base pairs, and\nthese will be used to generate at most n2 |B| new pairs that will be added to the\nset F. For each pair (U, S) ∈ B of type 1, we can compute pw(U, S) by dynamic\nprogramming on increasing size over the set of pairs of type 1. The procedure\nfor this is given in Equation 1. Put these pairs as the initial content of the set\nF. For all of these pairs, U is a full set. Notice that initially we cannot compute\nthe pathwidth of a type 2 pair (U, S). The function pw(U, S) 