{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YKM9okVE4ojcyryr85MbWp","type":"tool_result","content":"7 XP Algorithm\n\nIn this section, we develop an algorithm based on general commitments to prove\nLemma 7. This algorithm also immediately implies an XP algorithm for directed\npathwidth.\n   We need some tools for finding k-committable extensions and for deciding if a\ngiven extension is k-committable.\n\nLemma 10 Let σ be a k-feasible sequence for G and let τ be a k-feasible proper\nextension of σ . Let W be a vertex set with V (σ ) \u0002 W ⊆ V (τ ) such that d − (W ) ≤\nd − (X ) for every X with V (σ ) ⊆ X ⊆ V (τ ). Let τW denote the subsequence of τ such\nthat V (τW ) = W . Then, τW is a k-committable extension of σ .\n\nProof From the choice of W , we have d − (X ) ≥ d − (W ) for every X , V (σ ) ⊆ X ⊆ W .\nTherefore, it suffices to show that τW is k-feasible. Consider an arbitrary extension α\nof σ that is a prefix of τW . Our goal is to show that d − (α) ≤ k.\n   Let β be the shortest prefix of τ such that V (α) ⊆ V (β). By submodularity, we\nhave\n\n                   d − (β) + d − (W ) ≥ d − (α) + d − (V (β) ∪ W ),\n\nwhere we have used V (α) = V (β) ∩ W . Since β is a prefix of τ , we have d − (β) ≤\nk. Moreover, we have d − (W ) ≤ d − (V (β) ∪ W ) by the assumption on W since\nV (σ ) \u0002 V (β) ∪ W ⊆ V (τ ). Therefore, we have d − (α) ≤ k. Since this holds for\nevery extension α of σ that is a prefix of τW and σ is k-feasible, τW is k-feasible.\n\nCorollary 2 Let σ be a k-feasible sequence and let τ be a k-feasible proper extension\nof σ with d − (τ ) ≤ d − (σ ). Then, there exists a k-committable extension of σ that is\na subsequence of τ . Moreover, such a subsequence can be computed in O(km) time,\ngiven σ and τ , where m is the number of edges of G.\n\nProof Let W minimize d − (W ) subject to V (σ ) ⊆ W ⊆ V (τ ). We may choose W to\nbe a proper superset of V (σ ) since d − (τ ) ≤ d − (σ ). Lemma 10 applies and τW , the\nsubsequence of τ with V (τW ) = W , is a k-committable extension of σ .\n   To obtain such W , we define digraph G as follows: V (G ) = I ∪ {s, t}, where\ns and t are distinct vertices not in V (G) and I = V (τ ) ∪ N − (τ )\\V (σ ); E(G ) =\nE(G[I ]) ∪ {(s, v) | v ∈ N − (τ )} ∪ {(v, t) | v ∈ N − (σ )}. We say that S ⊆ I is an s–t\nseparator if there is no directed path from s to t in G [V (G )\\S].\n   Observe that, for every U such that V (σ ) ⊆ U ⊆ V (τ ), N − (U ) is an s–t separator.\nConversely, each minimal s–t separator S is N − (U ) for some V (σ ) ⊆ U ⊆ V (τ ).\nTo see this, let S be an arbitrary minimal s–t separator, let R S denote the set of\nvertices v ∈ I such that there is a directed path of G [V (G )\\S] from s to v, and\nlet U S = V (σ ) ∪ I \\(S ∪ R S ). Then, we have U S ⊆ V (τ ) since N − (τ ) ⊆ R S and\nV (τ ) = V (σ ) ∪ I \\N − (τ ). Therefore, N − (U S ) is an s–t separator. We must have\nN − (U S ) ⊆ S since a vertex v ∈ N − (U S )\\S ⊆ R S would provide a directed path\nfrom s to some vertex in U S , a contradiction. Since S is a minimal s–t separator, we\nconclude that S = N − (U S ).\n\n123\n\fAlgorithmica (2016) 75:138–157                                                     149\n\n\n   Thus, the problem of finding W such that V (σ ) ⊆ W ⊆ V (τ ) minimizing |N − (W )|\nreduces to that of finding a minimum s–t separator, which can be done by a standard\naugmenting path algorithm for the maximum number of internally disjoint s–t paths.\nIf we find d − (τ ) internally disjoint paths, then N − (τ ) is a minimum s–t separator\nand we are done. Otherwise, the algorithm finds a minimum s–t separator S with\n|S| < d − (τ ). We set W = U S . Since d − (V (σ )) ≥ d − (τ ) > |S|, we cannot have\nW = V (σ ) and therefore W is the required set. Since the disjoint paths computation\ntakes at most k augmenting steps and each step takes O(m) time, the time for this\ncomputation is O(km).\n   We formalize our algorithm as a recursive procedure Search below. We consider\nG and k fixed in the algorithm description. Procedure Search receives a non-negative\ninteger g ≤ k and a k-feasible sequence σ such that d − (σ ) ≥ k − g and returns one\nof the following:\n1. (SOLUTION τ ),\n2. FAILURE, or\n3. (COMMIT τ ).\n   When call Search(g, σ ) returns (SOLUTION τ ), τ is a k-feasible permutation\nthat extends σ ; when it returns FAILURE, there is no k-feasible permutation that\nextends σ ; when it returns (COMMIT τ ), τ is a k-committable extension of σ satis-\nfying d − (τ ) ≤ k − g.\n   We start with an auxiliary procedure Exhaust, which does an exhaustive search.\nProcedure Exhaust(σ )\nInput: a k-feasible sequence σ .\nOutput: (SOLUTION τ ), where τ is a k-feasible permutation that extends σ or\nFAILURE, when it is found that there is no k-feasible permutation that extends σ .\nSteps:\nCase E1: |σ | ≥ n − k − 1. Arbitrarily extend σ into a permutation σ and Return\n         (SOLUTION σ ).\nShell cwd was reset to /home/al/dev/MOSP","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3e233eac-acc6-45da-bdf4-c3a7e4de0805","uuid":"f273bc6f-15b1-4d88-8444-cec4461ba639","timestamp":"2026-10-03T01:19:21.382Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"7 XP Algorithm\n\nIn this section, we develop an algorithm based on general commitments to prove\nLemma 7. This algorithm also immediately implies an XP algorithm for directed\npathwidth.\n   We need some tools for finding k-committable extensions and for deciding if a\ngiven extension is k-committable.\n\nLemma 10 Let σ be a k-feasible sequence for G and let τ be a k-feasible proper\nextension of σ . Let W be a vertex set with V (σ ) \u0002 W ⊆ V (τ ) such that d − (W ) ≤\nd − (X ) for every X with V (σ ) ⊆ X ⊆ V (τ ). Let τW denote the subsequence of τ such\nthat V (τW ) = W . Then, τW is a k-committable extension of σ .\n\nProof From the choice of W , we have d − (X ) ≥ d − (W ) for every X , V (σ ) ⊆ X ⊆ W .\nTherefore, it suffices to show that τW is k-feasible. Consider an arbitrar